// AUTO-GENERATED deep explanations for the revamp engine (concept + per-wrong-option traps).
// Source: /tmp/deep_build/out_*.json (15 parallel writer agents). Merge: build in-session.
// Keyed by mock id -> question id. Rendered by explainHTML() in screen.html.
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"met-mock-2": {"m2_bio01":{"concept":"The resting membrane potential (RMP) is the steady voltage difference (about −70 mV inside relative to outside) that an unstimulated neuron maintains across its membrane. It exists because the membrane at rest is far more permeable to K+ than to Na+ (through open K+ leak channels), so K+ leaking outward sets the voltage near the K+ equilibrium potential, while the Na+/K+ ATPase pump (3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in per ATP) continually restores the concentration gradients that leak would otherwise dissipate. Option b captures both facts: selective K+ permeability sets the voltage and the pump preserves the gradients.","traps":{"a":"Equal Na+ and K+ permeability would drive the voltage to a value midway between their equilibrium potentials, near 0 mV, not −70 mV. This is the trap of ignoring that at rest K+ permeability greatly exceeds Na+ permeability.","c":"Cl− movement contributes only modestly and passively follows its equilibrium; there is no dedicated 'active extrusion of Cl− only' setting the RMP. It invents a pump that does not drive the resting potential.","d":"A continuous Ca2+ influx would depolarise the cell, the opposite of maintaining a negative resting state; voltage-gated Ca2+ channels are largely closed at rest. This confuses resting conditions with active signalling or synaptic events."}},"m2_bio02":{"concept":"Saltatory conduction is the fast, energy-efficient way action potentials travel down myelinated axons: the myelin sheath insulates the internodal segments so current cannot leak there, and voltage-gated Na+ channels are concentrated only at the gaps between myelin, the nodes of Ranvier. The impulse therefore regenerates only at successive nodes and appears to 'jump' (Latin <i>saltare</i>, to leap) from node to node, which option b states exactly.","traps":{"a":"A continuous, uniform distribution of Na+ channels describes unmyelinated (continuous) conduction, which is slower. It describes the very opposite of the nodal clustering that makes saltatory conduction possible.","c":"This reverses the myelinating cells: Schwann cells myelinate the peripheral nervous system and oligodendrocytes myelinate the central nervous system, not the other way around. Classic CNS/PNS glia reversal trap.","d":"Action potentials normally propagate unidirectionally from axon hillock toward the terminal, not bidirectionally along dendrites. This confuses passive dendritic signal spread with active axonal conduction."}},"m2_bio03":{"concept":"The brain has several dopamine pathways, each with a distinct origin, target, and function. The mesolimbic pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area (VTA) to the nucleus accumbens (ventral striatum) and drives reward, motivation, and addiction; its overactivity is the leading account of the positive symptoms of schizophrenia (hallucinations, delusions), which is why D2-blocking antipsychotics reduce them. Option d names both the correct target and the correct function.","traps":{"a":"The caudate-putamen and voluntary movement describe the nigrostriatal pathway (substantia nigra to dorsal striatum), whose degeneration causes Parkinson's disease. It swaps in the motor dopamine system.","b":"The median eminence and prolactin regulation describe the tuberoinfundibular pathway (arcuate nucleus to median eminence), which tonically inhibits prolactin. This is a different dopamine tract entirely.","c":"The substantia nigra is the origin, not the target, of the nigrostriatal pathway, and Parkinsonian rigidity reflects its loss, not mesolimbic activity. It confuses the movement pathway and its clinical failure with the reward pathway."}},"m2_bio04":{"concept":"The basal ganglia are a group of interconnected subcortical nuclei that regulate voluntary movement, habit, and action selection: the caudate nucleus and putamen (together the striatum), the globus pallidus, the subthalamic nucleus, and the substantia nigra. The red nucleus is NOT among them; it sits in the midbrain tegmentum and is the origin of the rubrospinal tract, part of a separate motor system, so option c is the correct 'not a component' answer.","traps":{"a":"The caudate nucleus is a core part of the striatum and thus a genuine basal ganglia component, so it cannot be the exception. Trap for those who forget the caudate belongs to the striatum.","b":"The globus pallidus (internal and external segments) is a central basal ganglia output structure. Naming it as the exception reflects unfamiliarity with the standard list.","d":"The subthalamic nucleus is a recognised basal ganglia component and a target of deep-brain stimulation in Parkinson's disease. It is a true member, not the outlier."}},"m2_bio05":{"concept":"Language in most people is lateralised to the dominant (usually left) hemisphere, and two classic regions divide the labour: Wernicke's area handles comprehension, Broca's area handles fluent production. Wernicke's area lies in the posterior superior temporal gyrus (Brodmann area 22); damage produces fluent but empty 'word-salad' speech with poor comprehension, which matches option b.","traps":{"a":"The inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere is Broca's area (BA 44/45), responsible for speech production, not Wernicke's. Classic Broca-for-Wernicke swap.","c":"The right parietal lobe is the non-dominant hemisphere and is linked to spatial attention and neglect, not to Wernicke's language comprehension. Wrong hemisphere and wrong function.","d":"The anterior cingulate cortex is involved in error monitoring, conflict, and emotion, not receptive language. It has nothing to do with Wernicke's area."}},"m2_bio06":{"concept":"The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis is the body's core stress hormone cascade: the hypothalamus releases CRH, which drives the anterior pituitary to release ACTH, which drives the adrenal cortex to release cortisol. Cortisol, the end product, feeds back negatively to shut down its own drivers, acting mainly on the hypothalamus (suppressing CRH) and the anterior pituitary (suppressing ACTH), which is exactly option a. Failure of this feedback (dexamethasone non-suppression) is a classic marker in melancholic depression.","traps":{"b":"The pineal gland secretes melatonin and the posterior pituitary stores oxytocin and vasopressin; neither is the target of cortisol's HPA feedback. It names endocrine structures unrelated to the HPA loop.","c":"The adrenal medulla secretes adrenaline as part of the sympathetic system, not cortisol (that is the adrenal cortex), and it is not the primary feedback site. This confuses the medulla with the cortex and misplaces feedback at the adrenal.","d":"The vagal motor nucleus belongs to parasympathetic autonomic control, unrelated to cortisol's endocrine negative feedback. It is an anatomically irrelevant distractor."}},"m2_bio07":{"concept":"Long-term potentiation (LTP) is the lasting strengthening of a synapse after high-frequency stimulation and is a leading cellular model of learning and memory, first described by Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo in 1973 in the hippocampus. The NMDA glutamate receptor makes LTP possible because it is a coincidence detector: at resting potential its channel is plugged by Mg2+, and only when the postsynaptic cell is depolarised (expelling the Mg2+) AND glutamate is bound does the channel open to admit Ca2+, the trigger for LTP. Option a states both requirements.","traps":{"b":"Glycine is a co-agonist that NMDA receptors also need, but glycine alone cannot open the channel; glutamate and depolarisation are still required. The trap overstates glycine's role and drops the coincidence requirement.","c":"NMDA receptors are excitatory (they admit Ca2+ and Na+), not inhibitory; inhibitory transmission is mediated by GABA and glycine receptors. This mislabels an excitatory receptor as inhibitory.","d":"NMDA receptors are postsynaptic ionotropic receptors; presynaptic vesicle fusion is triggered by Ca2+ entry through presynaptic voltage-gated channels, a separate event. It puts a postsynaptic receptor on the wrong side of the synapse."}},"m2_bio08":{"concept":"The hypothalamus contains opposing feeding centres. The lateral hypothalamus is the 'hunger/feeding centre' that drives eating, while the ventromedial nucleus is the 'satiety centre' that stops eating. Destroying the lateral hypothalamus removes the drive to eat, producing aphagia (refusal to eat) and weight loss, which is option d.","traps":{"a":"Hyperphagia and obesity follow lesions of the ventromedial (satiety) nucleus, not the lateral hypothalamus. This is the classic reversal of the two feeding centres.","b":"Persistent thirst relates to osmoregulatory and lateral preoptic mechanisms, and lateral hypothalamic lesions produce adipsia (loss of drinking) rather than persistent thirst. It swaps the actual effect for its opposite.","c":"Loss of circadian rhythm results from damage to the suprachiasmatic nucleus, the body's master clock, not the lateral hypothalamus. Right general region, wrong specific nucleus and function."}},"m2_bio09":{"concept":"Patient Henry Molaison (H.M.) had both medial temporal lobes, including most of the hippocampus, removed in 1953 to treat epilepsy, and his case is foundational to memory science. He could still hold information briefly (intact working memory) and could learn new motor skills like mirror-drawing (intact procedural memory), but he could no longer form new declarative memories, a profound anterograde amnesia. This showed the hippocampus is essential for consolidating new declarative (episodic and semantic) memories, which is option b, rather than being the site of permanent storage.","traps":{"a":"H.M. actually retained the ability to acquire procedural (skill) memories, improving on mirror-drawing despite not remembering practising; procedural learning was spared, not impaired. This states the opposite of his famous preserved ability.","c":"His working memory (immediate span) was intact; he could hold a number for a short time. The trap confuses short-term/working memory with the long-term consolidation that was lost.","d":"H.M. retained memories, including faces, from well before surgery; his deficit was forming NEW memories, not recognising old, pre-surgical ones. It mistakes anterograde amnesia for retrograde loss of old knowledge."}},"m2_bio10":{"concept":"Huntington's disease is an autosomal dominant neurodegenerative disorder caused by an expanded CAG trinucleotide repeat in the HTT (huntingtin) gene on chromosome 4p; the expanded repeat encodes a toxic polyglutamine stretch, and the disease shows anticipation (earlier, more severe onset across generations), especially with paternal transmission. Option d gives the exact mutation, gene, and chromosome.","traps":{"a":"A CGG trinucleotide expansion on the X chromosome describes Fragile X syndrome, not Huntington's. It swaps the CAG repeat for the CGG repeat of a different disorder.","b":"FMR1 silencing is the mechanism of Fragile X syndrome, where CGG expansion methylates and silences FMR1. Again this is Fragile X, not Huntington's.","c":"A paternal deletion on chromosome 15 causes Prader-Willi syndrome (maternal deletion of the same region causes Angelman). Wrong chromosome and wrong disease entirely."}},"m2_bio11":{"concept":"Sleep cycles through non-REM stages and REM (rapid eye movement) sleep, each with a signature EEG. REM sleep, when most vivid dreaming occurs, shows a paradoxically wake-like EEG (low-voltage, mixed-frequency, desaturated), combined with rapid darting eye movements and near-complete skeletal muscle atonia driven by the pons that prevents acting out dreams. Option b lists all three hallmarks.","traps":{"a":"Delta (slow) waves and the growth hormone surge belong to N3 slow-wave sleep, the deepest non-REM stage, not REM. It attaches deep non-REM features to REM.","c":"Sleep spindles and K-complexes are the defining features of N2 (stage 2) non-REM sleep, not REM. Classic N2-for-REM confusion.","d":"Alpha waves dominant in the occipital leads characterise relaxed wakefulness with eyes closed, not REM sleep. It mistakes a waking resting rhythm for a sleep stage."}},"m2_bio12":{"concept":"The corticospinal tract carries voluntary motor commands from the cerebral cortex to the spinal cord. About 85 to 90 percent of its fibres cross the midline (decussate) at the pyramidal decussation in the caudal medulla, forming the lateral corticospinal tract on the opposite side; this is why one hemisphere controls the opposite side of the body. Option c names this crossing point.","traps":{"a":"Only the smaller anterior (ventral) corticospinal tract stays uncrossed until it reaches its segment, and it is the minority of fibres; the main lateral tract crosses in the medulla, not 'at the level of entry'. It generalises the minor exception to the whole tract.","b":"The pons is where corticopontine and corticobulbar fibres pass, but it is not where the corticospinal tract decussates. Right brainstem neighbourhood, wrong crossing site.","d":"The cerebral peduncles of the midbrain carry the descending corticospinal fibres but the fibres travel through them without crossing; decussation happens lower, in the medulla. It confuses a passage point with the decussation point."}},"m2_bio13":{"concept":"The prefrontal cortex is the front-most part of the frontal lobe and governs 'executive' functions: planning, impulse control, judgement, and integrating emotion into decisions. The ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC) sits at the bottom-middle of this region and specifically links emotional/visceral signals to rational choice. In 1848 the tamping iron drove through Phineas Gage's skull and destroyed this area bilaterally; physician John Harlow documented the shift from responsible to impulsive and socially disinhibited, and Antonio Damasio's later somatic-marker hypothesis explained why vmPFC damage wrecks value-based decision-making. Option b names exactly that region.","traps":{"a":"The posterior parietal cortex handles spatial attention and sensorimotor integration; its damage causes neglect or apraxia, not the emotional/personality disinhibition Gage showed. Wrong lobe entirely.","c":"The hippocampus and amygdala underlie memory formation and fear/emotion processing; bilateral damage gives amnesia or Klüver-Bucy syndrome, not Gage's executive-judgement profile. Trap: these are 'emotion' structures, so they sound plausible, but Gage's deficit was decision-making, not raw emotion or memory.","d":"The cerebellar vermis governs balance, posture, and coordination; damage causes gait ataxia, not social disinhibition. Completely unrelated motor structure."}},"m2_bio14":{"concept":"The parasympathetic nervous system is the 'rest and digest' branch, and its cranial outflow travels on cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X. The vagus nerve (cranial nerve X) is by far the broadest: it carries parasympathetic fibres down into the thorax and abdomen, slowing the heart, driving bronchial secretion, and stimulating gut motility as far as the splenic flexure of the colon. Because it supplies most thoracic and abdominal viscera (not just head structures), option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"The trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve V) is primarily sensory to the face plus motor to the muscles of mastication; it carries essentially no visceral parasympathetic supply. Trap: it is a large cranial nerve, but its job is facial sensation, not visceral control.","b":"The glossopharyngeal nerve (cranial nerve IX) does carry parasympathetic fibres, but only to the parotid gland in the head; it does not reach thoracic or abdominal viscera. Trap: partially correct 'parasympathetic' label, wrong target territory.","d":"The accessory nerve (cranial nerve XI) is purely motor to the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles; it carries no parasympathetic fibres at all. Wrong function."}},"m2_bio15":{"concept":"Korsakoff's syndrome is a chronic amnestic disorder marked by dense anterograde amnesia and confabulation (unconsciously filling memory gaps with invented material). It is caused by deficiency of vitamin B1, thiamine, most often from chronic alcohol misuse, which damages the mammillary bodies and the dorsomedial nucleus of the thalamus. Because thiamine is the specific missing vitamin, option c is correct; untreated thiamine deficiency first produces acute Wernicke's encephalopathy (confusion, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia) that can progress to the chronic Korsakoff state.","traps":{"a":"Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord and a megaloblastic anaemia, plus its own dementia; it is not the cause of the classic Korsakoff amnestic picture. Trap: another B-vitamin that affects the nervous system, but the wrong one.","b":"Niacin (B3) deficiency causes pellagra, the triad of dermatitis, diarrhoea, and dementia; it is not linked to the mammillary-body amnesia of Korsakoff. Trap: also a B-vitamin with CNS effects, different syndrome.","d":"Folate (B9) deficiency causes megaloblastic anaemia and neural tube defects in pregnancy; it does not produce Korsakoff's syndrome. Wrong vitamin entirely."}},"m2_bio16":{"concept":"Hemispatial (unilateral) neglect is a failure to attend to, or act toward, one side of space, and it appears on the side opposite the brain lesion (contralesional). The right parietal lobe of the non-dominant hemisphere is specialised for spatial attention across both sides, so a right parietal lesion leaves the patient unable to attend to the LEFT half of space, causing left-sided neglect. Option d states this contralesional rule correctly. (A left parietal lesion rarely causes a lasting right neglect because the intact right hemisphere still covers both sides.)","traps":{"a":"Right-sided neglect would require a LEFT-hemisphere lesion, since neglect is contralateral; a right parietal lesion produces left neglect, not right. Trap: it reverses the contralesional rule.","b":"Aphasia is a language disorder from damage to the dominant (usually left) hemisphere's Broca's or Wernicke's areas; it is not a right-parietal sign. Trap: swaps the non-dominant spatial hemisphere for the dominant language one.","c":"Bilateral homonymous hemianopia is a visual-field loss from damage to both optic pathways/occipital cortices, not a parietal attention deficit; and neglect is not the same as a field cut. Trap: confuses a sensory visual loss with an attentional neglect."}},"m2_bio17":{"concept":"The retina has two photoreceptor types: rods, which are highly sensitive and drive low-light (scotopic) black-and-white vision, and cones, which need bright light and provide sharp, colour (photopic) vision. Cones are packed most densely at the fovea centralis, a tiny pit at the centre of the macula where acuity is highest and rods are essentially absent. Because the fovea is the point of maximal cone concentration, option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"The optic disc is where the optic nerve exits and blood vessels enter; it contains no photoreceptors at all and forms the physiological blind spot. Trap: it is a real retinal landmark, but the opposite of a cone-rich zone.","b":"The peripheral retina is dominated by rods (for dim-light and motion detection), with very few cones; acuity there is poor. Trap: reverses the rod/cone distribution.","c":"The ora serrata is the serrated anterior margin where the retina ends; it is far peripheral and not a region of cone concentration. Wrong location."}},"m2_bio18":{"concept":"Neurotransmitters are either excitatory (they make a target neuron more likely to fire) or inhibitory (less likely to fire). In the mature mammalian brain the principal inhibitory transmitter is gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA), which opens Cl− channels at GABA-A receptors to hyperpolarise neurons. Because GABA is the dominant brain-wide inhibitory transmitter, option a is correct; benzodiazepines and barbiturates work by potentiating GABA-A receptor activity. (Glycine is the main inhibitory transmitter in the spinal cord and brainstem, not the brain overall.)","traps":{"b":"Glutamate is the principal EXCITATORY transmitter in the brain, the functional opposite of what the question asks. Trap: it is the most abundant transmitter, so it tempts, but its role is excitation, not inhibition.","c":"Acetylcholine is a modulatory transmitter for muscle activation, arousal, attention, and memory; it is not the brain's main inhibitory transmitter. Wrong role.","d":"Norepinephrine is a modulatory arousal/alertness transmitter from the locus coeruleus; it is neither the dominant nor an inhibitory transmitter of the brain. Wrong category."}},"m2_bio19":{"concept":"In 1954 James Olds and Peter Milner discovered brain 'reward' or 'pleasure' centres by implanting stimulating electrodes in rats: when the electrode sat in the septal area and the medial forebrain bundle, rats would press a lever to self-stimulate thousands of times per hour, ignoring food and water to exhaustion. This pathway feeds the mesolimbic dopamine system that underlies modern reward circuitry. Option c names exactly the septal area / medial forebrain bundle they identified.","traps":{"a":"The cerebellar tonsils are part of the cerebellum (coordination/balance); herniation there is a neurological emergency, but they have nothing to do with reward self-stimulation. Wrong structure.","b":"The lateral geniculate nucleus is the thalamic relay for vision, not a reward centre. Trap: it is a named deep structure, but its role is sensory relay.","d":"The globus pallidus internus is a basal-ganglia output nucleus for motor control (a target in Parkinson's surgery), not the Olds-and-Milner pleasure centre. Wrong function."}},"m2_bio20":{"concept":"Motor control runs through two neurons: the upper motor neuron (UMN) descends from the motor cortex down the corticospinal tract, and the lower motor neuron (LMN) runs from the spinal cord/brainstem out to the muscle. Damaging the UMN removes descending inhibition, so signs are 'positive/spastic': hyperreflexia, increased tone (spasticity), and an extensor plantar (Babinski) response, with only mild disuse atrophy. Option c lists exactly this UMN picture. (LMN lesions instead give hyporeflexia, flaccidity, marked atrophy, and fasciculations.)","traps":{"a":"Fasciculations, atrophy, and hypotonia are LMN signs, not UMN; this option attaches LMN features to a UMN label. Trap: it swaps the two lesion types.","b":"Hyperreflexia is a UMN sign, not an LMN sign; LMN lesions cause reduced or absent reflexes. Trap: correct sign, wrong neuron assigned.","d":"Absent reflexes indicate an LMN lesion (the reflex arc's motor limb is cut); UMN lesions cause exaggerated, not absent, reflexes. Trap: reverses the reflex finding."}},"m2_bio21":{"concept":"Sleep cycles between non-REM stages (N1, N2, N3) and REM. N3 is slow-wave sleep, the deepest stage, dominated by high-amplitude delta waves and concentrated in the first third of the night. Parasomnias such as sleepwalking (somnambulism), night terrors, and confusional arousals are disorders of incomplete arousal OUT of this deep N3 stage, which is why they occur early in the night and are usually not remembered. Option d correctly names N3 slow-wave/delta sleep.","traps":{"a":"REM sleep is the home of vivid nightmares and REM sleep behaviour disorder (acting out dreams), not classic sleepwalking or night terrors; and during REM the body is normally paralysed (atonia), which prevents walking. Trap: both are 'sleep events', but they belong to different stages.","b":"N1 is the brief drowsy transition into sleep, associated with hypnic jerks, not the deep-sleep parasomnias of sleepwalking and terrors. Wrong stage.","c":"N2 is light sleep marked by sleep spindles and K-complexes; it is not the stage from which night terrors and sleepwalking emerge. Trap: it is a non-REM stage, but not the deep N3 that produces these events."}},"m2_bio22":{"concept":"The amygdala is an almond-shaped structure deep in the medial temporal lobe and is the brain's core emotional-salience and threat detector. Work by Joseph LeDoux mapped a fast subcortical 'low road' to the amygdala that drives fear conditioning, associating neutral cues with danger before the cortex fully processes them. Because its central function is fear conditioning and threat detection, option c is correct; bilateral amygdala damage produces the tameness and abnormal emotional reactions of Klüver-Bucy syndrome.","traps":{"a":"Verbal working memory is a dorsolateral prefrontal / phonological-loop function, not an amygdala role. Wrong system.","b":"Initiation of voluntary movement depends on the primary motor cortex, supplementary motor area, and basal ganglia, not the amygdala. Wrong structure.","d":"Long-term declarative memory storage is bound to the hippocampus and neocortex; the amygdala only modulates the emotional strength of such memories. Trap: the amygdala sits beside the hippocampus and influences memory, so it is easy to confuse the two roles."}},"m2_bio23":{"concept":"Dopamine reaches the forebrain by four main pathways, and antipsychotics block D2 receptors along all of them. The nigrostriatal pathway runs from the substantia nigra to the striatum and controls movement; blocking its D2 receptors mimics Parkinson's disease, producing the extrapyramidal side effects (EPS): parkinsonism, akathisia, acute dystonia, and, long-term, tardive dyskinesia. Because EPS come from nigrostriatal blockade, option b is correct.","traps":{"a":"The mesolimbic pathway's D2 blockade is what treats the positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions) of psychosis; it does not cause the movement EPS. Trap: it is the therapeutic target, not the source of motor side effects.","c":"The mesocortical pathway projects to the prefrontal cortex; blocking it tends to WORSEN negative and cognitive symptoms, not cause EPS. Wrong pathway/effect.","d":"The tuberoinfundibular pathway controls prolactin release; its D2 blockade raises prolactin (causing galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea), not extrapyramidal motor signs. Trap: a real antipsychotic side effect, but an endocrine one, not EPS."}},"m2_bio24":{"concept":"The thalamus is a paired egg-shaped mass at the centre of the brain that acts as the great sensory relay station: almost every sensory signal synapses in a thalamic nucleus before being sent on to the cortex. Vision relays through the lateral geniculate nucleus, hearing through the medial geniculate nucleus, and body sensation through the ventral posterior (VPL/VPM) nuclei. Because it relays nearly all modalities to cortex, option c is correct; olfaction is the single exception, projecting from the olfactory bulb straight to the piriform cortex without a thalamic relay.","traps":{"a":"The hypothalamus regulates homeostasis, hormones, hunger, temperature, and the autonomic system; it is not the sensory relay to cortex. Trap: similar name to thalamus, entirely different job.","b":"The basal ganglia modulate voluntary movement and habit learning; they are not a sensory relay to the cortex. Wrong function.","d":"The hippocampus forms new declarative memories; it receives highly processed input but does not relay raw sensory modalities to the cortex. Wrong role."}},"m2_bio25":{"concept":"The two cerebral hemispheres are joined by the corpus callosum, a thick band of about 200 million axons that lets them share information. Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga studied epilepsy patients whose corpus callosum had been surgically cut (commissurotomy) to control seizures. Because each hemisphere receives input from the opposite (contralateral) visual field, a picture flashed only to the left visual field reaches only the right hemisphere; the patient cannot name it (speech is usually left-lateralised) but can point to or draw it with the left hand. This proved that after the callosum is sectioned, each hemisphere perceives, learns, and responds on its own, which is exactly what option b states.","traps":{"a":"This is the opposite of the finding. Split-brain work showed language is strongly lateralised (usually left hemisphere), not fully bilateral; if it were bilateral, patients could name left-visual-field stimuli, which they could not.","c":"Reverses hemispheric dominance. In most right-handers the LEFT hemisphere controls speech, not the right; the mute right hemisphere is precisely why disconnected right-hemisphere stimuli could not be named.","d":"Denies the contralateral organisation of vision that the experiments relied on. Each visual field projects to the OPPOSITE hemisphere only; if both fields reached both hemispheres equally, the split-brain naming dissociation could not occur."}},"m2_bio26":{"concept":"The default mode network (DMN) is a set of interconnected brain regions, chiefly the medial prefrontal cortex, posterior cingulate cortex and precuneus, and the angular gyrus, that Marcus Raichle and colleagues identified as being active when a person is NOT engaged with the outside world. It ramps up during rest, mind-wandering, daydreaming, recalling autobiographical memories, imagining the future, and thinking about oneself or others' minds (theory of mind), and it deactivates when attention turns to an external task. Option a captures this signature: rest, mind-wandering, and self-referential thought.","traps":{"b":"Externally focused, goal-directed tasks are when the DMN switches OFF and the task-positive (dorsal attention/frontoparietal) network switches on. This describes the anticorrelated network, not the DMN.","c":"Motor execution engages the primary motor cortex, premotor cortex, cerebellum, and basal ganglia, not the DMN. It is an external, action-oriented task that suppresses default mode activity.","d":"Early visual processing occurs in the occipital primary visual cortex (V1) driven by external stimuli. This is sensory, stimulus-driven activity, the opposite of the internally-oriented DMN."}},"m2_bio27":{"concept":"Aphasias are language disorders from brain damage, and they are distinguished by three features: fluency of speech, comprehension, and ability to repeat. Transcortical motor aphasia looks like Broca's aphasia (sparse, non-fluent, effortful speech with good comprehension) but with one crucial difference: repetition is preserved. This happens because the lesion sits in the frontal cortex ANTERIOR or SUPERIOR to Broca's area (or disconnects it from the supplementary motor area) while Broca's area itself and its link to Wernicke's area via the arcuate fasciculus stay intact, so words can still be echoed. That matches option d.","traps":{"a":"The posterior superior temporal gyrus is Wernicke's area; damage there causes Wernicke's (receptive) aphasia with fluent but meaningless speech and poor comprehension, not the non-fluent transcortical motor pattern.","b":"The angular gyrus is linked to Gerstmann's syndrome (agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, left-right disorientation) and reading/writing deficits, not to transcortical motor aphasia.","c":"The arcuate fasciculus connects Broca's and Wernicke's areas; cutting it produces conduction aphasia, whose hallmark is POOR repetition. That is the exact opposite of transcortical motor aphasia, in which repetition is spared."}},"m2_bio28":{"concept":"The Papez circuit, proposed by James Papez in 1937, is a closed anatomical loop he theorised as the substrate of emotion. The pathway runs: hippocampus to fornix to mammillary bodies, then via the mammillothalamic tract to the anterior thalamic nucleus, on to the cingulate gyrus, through the cingulum to the entorhinal cortex, and back to the hippocampus. Option c reproduces this loop correctly. Paul MacLean later expanded these structures, adding the amygdala and others, into the broader 'limbic system'.","traps":{"a":"This traces a motor/cerebellar loop (thalamus, primary motor cortex, cerebellum). It involves movement control, not the limbic emotion circuit Papez described.","b":"This is essentially a basal ganglia circuit (caudate, putamen, globus pallidus) with the amygdala tacked on. The amygdala was a MacLean addition, and the basal ganglia govern movement, so this is not the Papez loop.","d":"Substantia nigra to striatum to globus pallidus is the nigrostriatal/basal ganglia motor pathway involved in Parkinson's disease, not the hippocampal emotion circuit of Papez."}},"m2_bio29":{"concept":"Antipsychotic drugs treat psychosis largely by blocking dopamine transmission. Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics such as haloperidol and chlorpromazine are potent antagonists at the dopamine D2 receptor, and Philip Seeman and Tyrone Lee showed that a drug's clinical potency correlates almost perfectly with its D2 binding affinity, the core evidence for the dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia. Hence option c, dopamine D2, is the primary target.","traps":{"a":"5-HT2A antagonism is the defining feature of ATYPICAL (second-generation) antipsychotics, thought to reduce extrapyramidal side effects; it is not the main mechanism of the typicals.","b":"D1 receptors are not the principal therapeutic target of typical antipsychotics; clinical potency tracks D2, not D1, affinity.","d":"Muscarinic M1 blockade by antipsychotics is a source of anticholinergic SIDE effects (dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision), not the antipsychotic mechanism of action."}},"m2_bio30":{"concept":"Cushing's syndrome is caused by chronically excess cortisol (hypercortisolism), whether from a tumour, adrenal overactivity, or steroid medication. Because cortisol acts widely on the brain, the syndrome commonly brings psychiatric and cognitive changes; the most frequent is depression (reported in roughly 50 to 80 percent of cases), often with anxiety, irritability, emotional lability, and impaired memory or concentration, and a minority develop mania or frank psychosis. Option c lists exactly this profile.","traps":{"a":"Mania can occur but is the exception, not the rule; the dominant picture is depression. Saying 'mania only' misstates the most common presentation.","b":"Catatonia is not a characteristic feature of Cushing's syndrome; it is seen in mood disorders, schizophrenia, and various medical/neurological conditions.","d":"A selective olfactory deficit is unrelated to hypercortisolism. Anosmia points to olfactory nerve, sinus, or specific frontal/temporal pathology, not Cushing's."}},"m2_dev01":{"concept":"Object permanence is the understanding that objects continue to exist even when they cannot be seen, heard, or touched. Jean Piaget placed its development across his sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years). Partial versions appear around 8 months, but infants still make the A-not-B error (searching where an object was previously hidden rather than where it was just moved). Piaget considered object permanence FULLY established, including the ability to track invisible displacements, only near the end of the sensorimotor stage at about 18 to 24 months, which is option c.","traps":{"a":"At 4 to 6 months infants do not yet search for a fully hidden object; out of sight is out of mind. This is far too early for even partial object permanence.","b":"8 to 10 months is when EARLY, partial object permanence emerges, but infants still fail invisible-displacement and A-not-B tasks, so it is not 'fully established'. This is the classic trap of stopping at the first sign rather than full mastery.","d":"By 3 years object permanence has long been mastered; this is past the sensorimotor stage and later than Piaget's stated completion."}},"m2_dev02":{"concept":"Conservation is the Piagetian understanding that a quantity stays the same despite changes in its appearance, for example that pouring water into a taller, thinner glass does not change the amount. This ability appears in the concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years), when children gain reversibility (mentally undoing an action) and decentration (attending to more than one dimension at once). So liquid conservation is mastered in the concrete operational stage, option a. Within this stage different conservations appear at different ages, an unevenness Piaget called horizontal decalage.","traps":{"b":"Preoperational children (about 2 to 7 years) FAIL conservation; they centre on one dimension (e.g. height of the water) and lack reversibility. This is the stage where the classic conservation error is demonstrated, not where it is mastered.","c":"The sensorimotor stage (birth to 2) is about coordinating senses and actions and achieving object permanence, long before a child can reason about conserved quantities.","d":"Formal operational thought (about 11+) concerns abstract and hypothetical reasoning; conservation of liquid is acquired earlier, in the concrete operational stage, so this over-shoots the correct stage."}},"m2_dev03":{"concept":"Lev Vygotsky argued that learning is social and that a child's readiness to learn is best measured not by what they can already do alone but by what they can achieve with help. The Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) is the gap between the child's ACTUAL developmental level (independent problem solving) and their POTENTIAL level (problem solving guided by a more knowledgeable other). Option c states this gap precisely. David Wood, Jerome Bruner, and Gail Ross (1976) later called the temporary support given within this zone 'scaffolding'.","traps":{"a":"Mental age minus chronological age relates to the IQ/mental-age tradition of Alfred Binet and William Stern, not to Vygotsky's socially-mediated learning concept.","b":"What a child can do alone is the ACTUAL developmental level, only the lower boundary of the ZPD. The ZPD is the distance beyond that reachable with guidance, so this describes just one edge, not the zone.","d":"A fixed genetic potential ceiling is a nativist/maturational idea; the ZPD is explicitly about socially-assisted, dynamic potential, not a genetic cap."}},"m2_dev04":{"concept":"Erik Erikson described eight psychosocial stages, each a 'crisis' or tension the person must resolve. Adolescence (roughly 12 to 18 years) centres on Identity versus Role Confusion: the teenager works out who they are, their values, and their place in society, and successful resolution yields the virtue of fidelity. That is option b.","traps":{"a":"Industry versus Inferiority is the school-age stage (about 6 to 12), where children develop competence through schoolwork and skills; it precedes adolescence.","c":"Intimacy versus Isolation is the young-adulthood stage (about 18 to 40), concerned with forming committed close relationships, which comes after the adolescent identity crisis.","d":"Initiative versus Guilt is the preschool stage (about 3 to 6), about asserting control and purpose through play; it is much earlier than adolescence."}},"m2_dev05":{"concept":"Mary Ainsworth's Strange Situation is a laboratory procedure assessing infant attachment through a series of separations and reunions with the caregiver. A securely attached infant (type B, about 65 percent) uses the caregiver as a secure base to explore, shows mild distress when the caregiver leaves, and is readily comforted and returns to play on reunion. That description matches option a exactly, signalling a caregiver who has been consistently responsive.","traps":{"b":"Insecure-avoidant (type A) infants show LITTLE distress at separation and actively avoid or ignore the caregiver on reunion. The stem's easy soothing and secure-base exploration do not fit this pattern.","c":"Insecure-resistant/ambivalent (type C) infants are very distressed at separation and, on reunion, seek contact yet resist it (arching away, not settling). The stem says the infant is EASILY soothed, which contradicts resistance.","d":"Disorganised (type D, Mary Main and Judith Solomon, 1990) infants show contradictory, confused, or fearful behaviours with no coherent strategy. The stem describes a coherent, well-regulated pattern, i.e. secure."}},"m2_dev06":{"concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg described moral reasoning developing through three levels of two stages each. The pre-conventional level (typical of young children) judges right and wrong by external consequences to the self: Stage 1 is obedience and avoiding punishment, and Stage 2 is instrumental self-interest ('what's in it for me', simple exchange). Option d, obedience to authority and self-interest orientation, captures both pre-conventional stages.","traps":{"a":"Universal ethical principles define Stage 6 of the POST-conventional level, the highest stage of moral reasoning, not the pre-conventional level.","b":"Social contract reasoning is Stage 5, also POST-conventional, where laws are seen as flexible social agreements serving the greater good; this is well beyond pre-conventional.","c":"Conformity to interpersonal expectations ('good boy/good girl') is Stage 3 of the CONVENTIONAL level, based on gaining approval, not the self-interest/punishment focus of the pre-conventional level."}},"m2_dev07":{"concept":"Attachment is the enduring emotional bond between an infant and caregiver. An older behaviourist view (the 'cupboard love' or secondary-drive theory) held that infants bond with whoever feeds them, because the mother becomes associated with the reduction of the hunger drive. Harry Harlow tested this in the 1950s by raising infant rhesus monkeys with two surrogate 'mothers': a bare wire model that dispensed milk, and a soft cloth-covered model with no food. The infants clung to the soft cloth surrogate almost all day and only briefly visited the wire one to feed, and ran to the cloth mother when frightened. This shows attachment is driven by <b>contact comfort</b> (the reassurance of soft touch) rather than by feeding, making option b correct and supporting John Bowlby's ethological model over drive-reduction theory.","traps":{"a":"This is the secondary-drive / 'cupboard love' theory that Harlow's study specifically refuted; the monkeys chose the non-feeding cloth mother, so food was NOT the most powerful driver of attachment. Trap: states the very hypothesis the experiment disproved.","c":"Imprinting (rapid bonding in a fixed critical period) was demonstrated by Konrad Lorenz in geese, not by Harlow, and it applies to precocial birds, not primates. Trap: imports a different attachment researcher and phenomenon.","d":"Harlow's later isolation studies showed maternal deprivation produced severe, lasting social and emotional damage even when nutrition was adequate; the claim is the opposite of his findings. Trap: contradicts the deprivation results."}},"m2_dev08":{"concept":"Theory of mind is the ability to attribute mental states (beliefs, desires, intentions) to oneself and others, and to understand that another person's beliefs can differ from reality and from one's own. A false-belief task tests this directly: in the Sally-Anne test, Sally puts a marble in her basket and leaves; Anne moves it to a box; the child is asked where Sally will look. Passing requires the child to represent Sally's false belief (she still thinks it is in the basket) rather than reporting where the marble actually is. Children typically pass by about age 4, and children with autism show characteristic difficulty (Simon Baron-Cohen, Alan Leslie, and Uta Frith, 1985), so option a is correct.","traps":{"b":"Working memory is the capacity to hold and manipulate information briefly, tested by tasks like digit span or n-back, not by tracking another person's belief. Trap: a cognitive capacity unrelated to belief attribution.","c":"Conservation of number (understanding quantity stays the same despite rearrangement) is a Jean Piaget concrete-operational task, not a mentalising task. Trap: swaps in a Piagetian task from a different domain.","d":"Object permanence (knowing objects continue to exist when out of sight) is a sensorimotor achievement measured by hidden-object search, not by reasoning about someone else's belief. Trap: confuses representing a hidden object with representing a hidden mental state."}},"m2_dev09":{"concept":"Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess, in the New York Longitudinal Study, identified temperament as the biologically based, early-appearing style of how a child behaves. They described three broad types across dimensions such as activity level, rhythmicity, approach/withdrawal to novelty, adaptability, and intensity of reaction. The child in the stem shows initial withdrawal from new situations, slow but eventual adaptation, mild reaction intensity, and low-to-moderate activity, which is the <b>slow-to-warm-up</b> profile (option d): a cautious child who warms up gradually with repeated, unpressured exposure.","traps":{"a":"The 'easy' child has positive mood, regular biological rhythms, approaches novelty, and adapts quickly, not initial withdrawal and slow adaptation. Trap: matches on 'mild reactions' but ignores the withdrawal and slow adaptation.","b":"The 'difficult' child shows withdrawal and slow adaptation too, but with intense, negative reactions and irregular rhythms, whereas the stem specifies mild reactions and low activity. Trap: shares the withdrawal cue but the reaction intensity is opposite.","c":"'Inconsistent' is not one of Thomas and Chess's three categories (the roughly 35% who did not fit any type were labelled 'unclassified' in the study). Trap: a plausible-sounding but non-existent category."}},"m2_dev10":{"concept":"James Marcia operationalised Erik Erikson's identity-versus-role-confusion stage into four identity statuses defined by two variables: exploration (actively questioning and trying out alternatives) and commitment (settling on values, goals, or roles). Foreclosure is <b>commitment without exploration</b> (option a): the adolescent commits firmly, but to values simply handed down (usually by parents or authority) without ever exploring alternatives for themselves. The other three: moratorium is exploration without commitment; identity achievement is exploration followed by commitment; and diffusion is neither exploration nor commitment.","traps":{"b":"Exploration without commitment defines the moratorium status, an active crisis phase of searching before settling. Trap: swaps the two Marcia statuses that look similar.","c":"Both exploration and commitment define identity achievement, the mature endpoint reached after a genuine search. Trap: describes the most developed status, not foreclosure.","d":"Neither exploration nor commitment defines diffusion, the least developed status with no direction and no crisis. Trap: describes the opposite pole from foreclosure."}},"m2_dev11":{"concept":"Urie Bronfenbrenner's bioecological model nests a child's development inside layered environmental systems. The microsystem is any single setting the child directly participates in (home, one classroom, a peer group). The mesosystem is the set of <b>connections between two or more microsystems</b> the child belongs to, such as the relationship between family and school. A parent-teacher meeting or how home life affects school behaviour lives in the mesosystem, so the school-family relationship is option a.","traps":{"b":"The microsystem is a single direct setting (the school by itself, or the family by itself), not the relationship linking two of them. Trap: names one layer inward; a link between microsystems is the mesosystem.","c":"The exosystem is a setting that affects the child without their direct participation, such as a parent's workplace or a school board. Trap: the child is a direct member of both home and school, so it is not exosystem.","d":"The macrosystem is the overarching cultural context, ideology, laws, and customs, not a specific school-family link. Trap: jumps to the broadest cultural layer."}},"m2_dev12":{"concept":"The visual cliff, built by Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk (1960), is a table with a glass surface: one half has a checkerboard directly under the glass (the 'shallow' side) and the other half has the checkerboard dropped far below, creating the illusion of a cliff edge, though the glass is safe to crawl on. Infants old enough to crawl (about 6 months plus) refused to cross onto the apparent deep side even when coaxed by their mothers, and many young animals avoided it too. This indicates that <b>depth perception is largely present by the time independent locomotion begins</b> (option a).","traps":{"b":"Imprinting on caregivers is Konrad Lorenz's phenomenon in birds and was not what the visual cliff tested; the apparatus measured depth avoidance, not bonding. Trap: imports an unrelated attachment concept.","c":"Object permanence (objects persist when unseen) is a Jean Piaget concept tested by hidden-object search, not by the drop-avoidance of the visual cliff. Trap: swaps in a different infant-cognition milestone.","d":"The visual cliff is a purely visual depth task and says nothing about integrating audio with vision. Trap: invents a sensory-integration claim the study never addressed."}},"m2_dev13":{"concept":"The Babinski reflex is a primitive reflex in which stroking the sole of the foot makes the big toe extend upward and the other toes fan out. It is normal in infancy because the corticospinal (pyramidal) tract, which normally suppresses this response, is still immature and not yet fully myelinated. As myelination completes, the response flips to the adult (downgoing) pattern; the upgoing reflex is considered normal until roughly 12 months of age (option b). Its persistence beyond about age 2, or its reappearance in an adult, signals an upper-motor-neuron lesion.","traps":{"a":"3 months is too early; the extensor plantar response remains normal well past this, until about a year. Trap: undershoots the myelination timeline.","c":"6 months is still within the normal window but not the standard upper limit; the reflex normally persists to roughly 12 months. Trap: a plausible middle value that is early.","d":"By 24 months the reflex should have disappeared; persistence to this age suggests a corticospinal (UMN) abnormality rather than normal development. Trap: names the pathological-persistence threshold as if it were normal."}},"m2_dev14":{"concept":"Noam Chomsky argued that children cannot learn the complex, rule-governed structure of language purely from imitation and reinforcement, because the speech they hear is fragmentary, error-filled, and finite yet they come to produce and understand an unlimited number of novel, grammatical sentences (the 'poverty of the stimulus' argument, developed in his 1959 review of B. F. Skinner's Verbal Behavior). He proposed an innate Language Acquisition Device, a biologically prepared capacity encoding a Universal Grammar. So his theory most directly claims children possess an <b>innate, biologically prepared capacity for grammar</b> (option c).","traps":{"a":"Learning language purely by operant conditioning is B. F. Skinner's position, exactly the view Chomsky attacked. Trap: states the opposing behaviourist theory.","b":"Chomsky held that grammar is acquired without explicit teaching; children extract rules that parents themselves cannot articulate. Trap: contradicts the nativist claim that grammar is not taught.","d":"A claim about bilingual children lagging is empirically false and is not part of Chomsky's theory, which if anything predicts children can acquire multiple languages naturally. Trap: an unrelated, incorrect side-claim."}},"m2_dev15":{"concept":"Mary Ainsworth's Strange Situation originally sorted infants into three organised attachment patterns: secure (B), insecure-avoidant (A), and insecure-resistant/ambivalent (C), each a coherent strategy for managing the caregiver's presence and absence. Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1990) noticed some infants fit none of these because they showed no consistent strategy: on reunion they displayed <b>contradictory, freezing, dazed, or apprehensive behaviour</b> (for example approaching then abruptly avoiding the parent). They added the disorganised/disoriented category (D), which is over-represented in samples with caregiver maltreatment or unresolved parental trauma, making option b correct.","traps":{"a":"Consistent avoidance of the caregiver defines the organised insecure-avoidant type (A), which is a coherent strategy, not disorganisation. Trap: names an existing organised category.","c":"Easy soothing on reunion is the hallmark of secure attachment (B), the opposite of the distress and confusion seen in disorganised infants. Trap: describes secure attachment.","d":"Resistance and ambivalence, typically with distress rather than none, define the insecure-resistant type (C); disorganisation is marked by breakdown of any strategy, not resistance. Trap: points at the resistant category and even misstates it (C infants are highly distressed)."}},"m2_dev16":{"concept":"Jean Piaget's stage theory ends with the formal operational stage, beginning around age 11, in which thinking becomes abstract and no longer tied to concrete objects. Its signature is hypothetico-deductive reasoning: the adolescent generates hypotheses ('if this variable matters, then...') and tests them systematically. Piaget demonstrated this with the pendulum problem, where formal-operational children isolate variables one at a time to find what determines the swing rate, so option a is correct.","traps":{"b":"The preoperational stage (about 2 to 7) is marked by symbolic thought, egocentrism, and lack of conservation, not systematic hypothesis testing. Trap: far too early a stage for abstract reasoning.","c":"The concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11) allows logical reasoning but only about concrete, present objects, not abstract hypotheticals. Trap: the stage just before formal operations, close but limited to the concrete.","d":"The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2) is about learning through senses and action, culminating in object permanence, with no verbal or abstract reasoning. Trap: the earliest stage, entirely pre-verbal."}},"m2_dev17":{"concept":"Cross-cultural work using Mary Ainsworth's Strange Situation shows secure attachment is the most common pattern worldwide, but the distribution of insecure types varies with childrearing values. In Karin and Klaus Grossmann's northern German samples, the insecure-avoidant type (A) was over-represented. This is interpreted as a cultural pattern, not pathology: German parents valued early independence and self-reliance, so infants showed less overt distress at separation and less reunion-seeking, behaviour the coding scheme reads as avoidant (option a). For contrast, Japanese samples often over-represent resistant (C) because infants there rarely experience separation and become intensely distressed.","traps":{"b":"Attachment is not genetically 'less' in German infants; the difference reflects rearing practices and how behaviour is coded, not innate bonding capacity. Trap: a biological-determinism claim with no evidence.","c":"The elevated avoidance came from a normal Strange Situation, not a modified protocol; the same standardised procedure was used. Trap: attributes the result to method rather than culture.","d":"Infants do not skip developmental attachment phases into adult attachment; this is a nonsensical developmental leap. Trap: an implausible distractor with no theoretical basis."}},"m2_dev18":{"concept":"Margaret Mahler described how the infant psychologically 'hatches' from a fused state with the mother into a separate self, in the separation-individuation process. Its third subphase, rapprochement (about 15 to 24 months), is marked by ambivalence: the toddler, now mobile and aware of being separate, alternates between confidently exploring away from the mother and returning to her for reassurance and 'emotional refuelling'. This oscillation between independence and reconnection is option d. Full object constancy (a stable internal image of the mother) consolidates later, by about 36 months.","traps":{"a":"Symbiotic merger with the mother describes Mahler's earlier symbiotic phase (about 2 to 5 months), before the toddler experiences itself as separate. Trap: names an earlier Mahler phase.","b":"Stranger anxiety onset (around 8 months) is a separate attachment milestone tied to the differentiation subphase, not the defining feature of rapprochement. Trap: a real milestone but from a different, earlier point.","c":"Full object constancy is the achievement of the fourth and final subphase (about 24 to 36 months), which comes after rapprochement, not during it. Trap: names the outcome that rapprochement leads toward but has not yet reached."}},"m2_dev19":{"concept":"The still-face paradigm was developed by Edward Tronick in 1978 to probe how infants regulate emotion in interaction. A caregiver first plays normally, then holds a blank, motionless, unresponsive face for about two minutes. Infants first escalate their bids (smiling, pointing, vocalising, reaching) to re-engage the parent, then, when reciprocity fails, they turn away, grimace, and become distressed. This shows that even very young infants (option c) are active partners who expect and seek contingent, back-and-forth affective exchange, not passive receivers.","traps":{"a":"This is the exact view the paradigm refutes. The infant's escalating attempts to repair the interaction prove they are active agents, not passive recipients of the caregiver's mood.","b":"A distractor about the timing of face recognition. The still-face effect depends on infants already tracking the caregiver's expression well before 12 months, and the paradigm was never designed to date facial recognition.","d":"Reverses the actual finding. Distressed infants seek the familiar caregiver, not strangers; preferring strangers when distressed would signal disordered attachment, which is not what the still-face demonstrates."}},"m2_dev20":{"concept":"Infant developmental testing requires instruments that do not rely on verbal instruction, because babies cannot yet follow spoken directions. The Bayley Scales of Infant and Toddler Development, originally by Nancy Bayley (current edition Bayley-4), assess children from birth to 42 months across cognitive, language, motor, social-emotional, and adaptive domains through structured play and observation. Its birth-to-42-month range across cognitive, language, and motor domains matches the stem exactly, making option d correct.","traps":{"a":"The Wechsler Preschool and Primary Scale of Intelligence (WPPSI) begins at 2 years 6 months, so it cannot assess from birth. It is a preschool IQ test, not an infant developmental scale.","b":"The McCarthy Scales of Children's Abilities span roughly 2 years 6 months to 8 years 6 months, again starting far past birth, and target preschool/early-school children.","c":"The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale (current edition) begins at age 2 and is a general intelligence test, not a birth-onwards multi-domain infant scale."}},"m2_dev21":{"concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg's theory of moral development has three levels (preconventional, conventional, postconventional), each with two stages. Stage 3, the first conventional stage, is called the 'good boy / nice girl' orientation: right action is what pleases others and earns their approval, so the child conforms to interpersonal expectations and values being seen as a good person. That emphasis on approval and meeting others' expectations is option d.","traps":{"a":"Avoiding punishment defines Stage 1 (obedience and punishment orientation) at the preconventional level, which precedes any concern with social approval.","b":"Universal ethical principles define Stage 6, the highest postconventional stage, where conscience follows self-chosen abstract principles, not the approval of close others.","c":"Maintaining social order through rules, law, and authority defines Stage 4, the second conventional stage. The trap is that Stage 4 broadens Stage 3's interpersonal focus to the whole society; the stem specifically asks about Stage 3."}},"m2_dev22":{"concept":"Developmental milestones follow a predictable sequence, so ordering them by typical age of emergence is a standard task. The social smile appears around 2 months, stranger anxiety around 8-10 months (peaking 12-15 months), and the pincer grasp around 9-12 months. Symbolic (pretend) play, in which a child uses one object to stand for another, emerges around 18-24 months and elaborates through the preschool years, making it the last of the four to appear (option b).","traps":{"a":"The social smile is one of the earliest milestones, at roughly 2 months, so it appears first, not last.","c":"Stranger anxiety emerges around 8-10 months, well before symbolic play; it reflects the clear-cut attachment phase, not later cognitive development.","d":"The pincer grasp (thumb-and-forefinger) is a fine-motor skill of about 9-12 months, still earlier than symbolic play."}},"m2_dev23":{"concept":"John Bowlby described attachment as unfolding in four phases. In the pre-attachment phase (0-2 months) the infant responds indiscriminately; in attachment-in-the-making (2-6 months) they begin recognising and preferring familiar caregivers; in the clear-cut attachment phase (roughly 6 months to 2 years) they show unmistakable preference for the primary caregiver, protest at separation, and display stranger anxiety; and after about 2 years comes the goal-corrected partnership. The stem's description of clear preference plus stranger anxiety matches the 6-months-to-2-years window, option c.","traps":{"a":"0-2 months is the pre-attachment phase, in which the infant responds indiscriminately and shows no focused preference or stranger anxiety.","b":"2-6 months is attachment-in-the-making: the infant starts to recognise familiar people but has not yet formed the clear, protest-driven attachment described in the stem.","d":"2-4 years falls in the goal-corrected partnership phase, when the child understands the caregiver's separate goals and negotiates; stranger anxiety has typically waned by then."}},"m2_dev24":{"concept":"Diana Baumrind classified parenting along two independent dimensions: demandingness (control, expectations, structure) and responsiveness (warmth, acceptance, sensitivity). Crossing them gives four styles. Authoritative parenting is high on both, combining firm, consistent standards with warmth and open communication, and is linked to the best child outcomes, so option a is correct.","traps":{"b":"High demandingness with low responsiveness defines the authoritarian style, which is controlling and cold rather than warm and reasoned.","c":"Low demandingness with high responsiveness defines the permissive (indulgent) style, warm but lacking limits.","d":"Low demandingness with low responsiveness defines the uninvolved (neglectful) style, associated with the poorest outcomes."}},"m2_dev25":{"concept":"In Jean Piaget's preoperational stage (about 2-7 years), thinking has characteristic limits. Centration is the tendency to focus on one perceptually salient dimension of a situation while ignoring the others. In the classic conservation-of-liquid task, the child fixates on the height of liquid in a tall thin glass and ignores its narrower width, so they wrongly judge it to hold more, which is exactly the 'focus on one feature' described in option c.","traps":{"a":"Inability to mentally reverse operations is irreversibility, a distinct preoperational limitation. The trap is that both irreversibility and centration cause conservation failure, but the stem defines centration specifically.","b":"The belief that inanimate objects have feelings or life is animism, another preoperational trait, not centration.","d":"Trouble taking another's spatial or mental viewpoint is egocentrism (shown in the three-mountains task), a separate preoperational feature, not the single-dimension focus the stem describes."}},"m2_abn01":{"concept":"DSM-5-TR sets duration criteria to separate schizophrenia from shorter psychotic disorders. Schizophrenia requires at least two of five core symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech, grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour, negative symptoms), with at least one being delusions, hallucinations, or disorganised speech, present for a significant portion of at least one month of active-phase symptoms, and continuous signs of disturbance for at least six months. Option a states both thresholds correctly.","traps":{"b":"One week is not a DSM duration threshold for any psychotic disorder; it undershoots even brief psychotic disorder's framing and confuses the reader with an arbitrary short period.","c":"Three months matches nothing here. It may lure those half-remembering the 1-6 month band, but schizophreniform disorder is 1-6 months and schizophrenia needs 6 months total.","d":"Twelve months overshoots. No psychotic-disorder criterion requires a full year; the six-month continuous-disturbance rule is the correct upper anchor."}},"m2_abn02":{"concept":"Eugen Bleuler coined the term 'schizophrenia' in 1911 and argued its fundamental (primary) features were four 'A's: loosening of Associations (disordered thought links), flattened Affect, Ambivalence (simultaneous contradictory feelings/impulses), and Autism (withdrawal into a private inner world). He regarded hallucinations and delusions as 'accessory' rather than core, an emphasis later reversed by Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms. Option b lists these four A's accurately.","traps":{"a":"Affect, Anhedonia, Avolition, and Alogia are modern negative symptoms of schizophrenia, not Bleuler's four A's. The trap is that they all start with 'A' and sound plausible.","c":"Apathy, Anxiety, Anger, and Aphasia are a made-up 'A' list; aphasia in particular is a neurological language disorder, not a Bleulerian concept.","d":"Audible thoughts, Anhedonia, Anosognosia, and Asociality mix Schneiderian first-rank content (audible thoughts) with other terms and are not Bleuler's scheme."}},"m2_abn03":{"concept":"Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) is a chronic low-grade depression defined by its long duration rather than episode severity. DSM-5-TR requires depressed mood most of the day, more days than not, for at least two years in adults, plus at least two additional symptoms (appetite, sleep, energy, self-esteem, concentration, or hopelessness), with no symptom-free interval longer than two months. In children and adolescents the requirement is one year and mood may present as irritability, so option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"Six months is far too short; it is roughly the GAD threshold and does not meet the chronic-course definition of PDD.","b":"One year is the requirement for children and adolescents only, not for adults, so as a general adult answer it is wrong.","c":"Five years is not a DSM threshold for PDD; it overshoots the required two years and confuses duration with something like chronic specifiers."}},"m2_abn04":{"concept":"The bipolar disorders are distinguished by the type of high-mood episode a person has ever had. A manic episode is an abnormally elevated or irritable mood with increased energy lasting at least seven days (or any duration if hospitalisation is required, or when psychosis is present); a hypomanic episode is milder and lasts at least four days. Bipolar I is defined by at least one lifetime manic episode, which is exactly what separates it from bipolar II, making option b correct.","traps":{"a":"A major depressive episode is required for bipolar II but is not what defines bipolar I; a person can be diagnosed with bipolar I without ever having a depressive episode, so this does not distinguish the two.","c":"Rapid cycling (four or more mood episodes in a year) is a course specifier that can apply to either bipolar I or II, so it does not separate them.","d":"Psychotic features are not the dividing line; psychosis can occur in bipolar I mania and its severe depression, so its absence does not distinguish bipolar I from II."}},"m2_abn05":{"concept":"Generalised anxiety disorder is marked by excessive, hard-to-control worry about multiple everyday matters. DSM-5-TR requires that this worry occur more days than not for at least six months and be accompanied by at least three of six symptoms (restlessness, easy fatigue, difficulty concentrating, irritability, muscle tension, sleep disturbance). The six-month threshold, also used in ICD-11, makes option b correct.","traps":{"a":"One month is too brief for GAD; it may echo the active-phase duration of schizophrenia or acute stress reactions but does not meet the GAD criterion.","c":"Three months is not a GAD threshold; it is a plausible-sounding distractor that undershoots the required six months.","d":"Twelve months overshoots the criterion. GAD needs only six months of excessive worry, not a full year."}},"m2_abn06":{"concept":"Delusional misidentification syndromes are rare psychotic phenomena where a patient misidentifies people, places, or objects while holding an unshakeable false belief about identity. Capgras syndrome, named after French psychiatrist Joseph Capgras, is the belief that a familiar person (spouse, parent) has been secretly replaced by an identical-looking impostor or double; the face is recognised but the felt sense of familiarity is lost, so the person is judged a fake. Option a states exactly this, so it is correct.","traps":{"b":"Describes a persecutory delusion, the core of paranoid presentations in schizophrenia or delusional disorder, not a misidentification syndrome. It confuses a delusion of persecution with a delusion of misidentification.","c":"This is Fregoli syndrome, the mirror-image of Capgras, where a patient believes strangers are actually one familiar persecutor in disguise. The trap swaps Capgras for its inverse (Fregoli).","d":"Describes a nihilistic or somatic delusion closer to Cotard-type body denial, not the impostor belief. It confuses a delusion about one's own body with misidentification of another person."}},"m2_abn07":{"concept":"Kurt Schneider proposed a set of first-rank symptoms (FRS) that, in the absence of organic disease, he considered strongly suggestive of schizophrenia. They centre on loss of ego boundaries: audible thoughts (Gedankenlautwerden), voices arguing or commenting, thought insertion/withdrawal/broadcast, somatic passivity, made affect/impulse/volition, and delusional perception. Loose associations (option a) are a formal thought disorder described by Eugen Bleuler as one of his fundamental primary symptoms (the four A's), not a Schneiderian first-rank symptom, so it is the correct EXCEPT answer.","traps":{"b":"Audible thoughts (Gedankenlautwerden) is a genuine Schneiderian first-rank symptom, so it is NOT the exception. The trap rewards anyone who does not recognise the German term as an FRS.","c":"Thought insertion is a classic passivity FRS (external agency placing thoughts in one's mind), so it belongs on Schneider's list and is not the exception.","d":"Delusional perception (a real perception given an abnormal delusional meaning) is one of the most specific Schneiderian first-rank symptoms, so it is not the exception."}},"m2_abn08":{"concept":"Post-traumatic stress disorder in DSM-5 is organised into symptom clusters that a patient must draw from after exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence. DSM-5 split the old DSM-IV avoidance/numbing cluster into two, producing four clusters: B intrusion (re-experiencing), C avoidance, D negative alterations in cognition and mood, and E alterations in arousal and reactivity, all lasting at least one month. Option d correctly names four clusters and lists them.","traps":{"a":"Two clusters undercounts the DSM-5 structure and matches no edition. It plays on vaguely recalling only intrusion and avoidance.","b":"Three clusters was closer to the DSM-IV grouping (re-experiencing, avoidance/numbing, hyperarousal) before DSM-5 divided avoidance/numbing. The trap rewards someone using the outdated DSM-IV count.","c":"Five clusters overcounts; five is the number of symptom categories in acute stress disorder, not the number of PTSD clusters. It confuses ASD's structure with PTSD's."}},"m2_abn09":{"concept":"Acute stress disorder (ASD) and PTSD describe the same kind of trauma response but are separated mainly by timing. ASD is diagnosed when the characteristic symptoms last from three days up to one month after the traumatic event; if clinically significant symptoms persist beyond one month the diagnosis becomes PTSD. Option c states this defining duration window, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"DSM-5 explicitly dropped any requirement that ASD must include dissociative symptoms; dissociation is one possible symptom category, not mandatory. The trap exploits the old belief that ASD is essentially a dissociative reaction.","b":"Onset in childhood is irrelevant to either diagnosis; both are keyed to a recent index trauma, not to age of first symptoms. It invents a distinction that does not exist.","d":"Both ASD and PTSD are classified in the Trauma- and Stressor-Related Disorders chapter, not the Dissociative Disorders chapter. The trap plays on the mistaken link between ASD and dissociation."}},"m2_abn10":{"concept":"A key way to tell obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) from obsessive-compulsive personality disorder (OCPD) is how the person experiences their own symptoms. In OCD the obsessions and compulsions are ego-dystonic: they feel intrusive, unwanted, senseless, and distressing, and the person typically resists them. In OCPD the perfectionism, rigidity, and control are ego-syntonic: they feel right and reasonable to the person. Option a correctly identifies OCD symptoms as ego-dystonic.","traps":{"b":"Ego-syntonic describes OCPD traits, not OCD symptoms; the person with OCPD sees their rigidity as correct. The trap reverses the two disorders.","c":"OCD content is often recognised by the patient as excessive or unrealistic, and insight can even be poor or absent; being 'always reality-grounded' is not the defining feature. It substitutes a false absolute for the real distinction.","d":"OCD commonly begins in adolescence or adulthood and is not restricted to childhood. The trap offers a plausible-sounding but incorrect developmental claim."}},"m2_abn11":{"concept":"Conversion disorder, now called functional neurological symptom disorder, involves one or more symptoms of altered voluntary motor or sensory function (weakness, paralysis, non-epileptic seizures, blindness) that are not explained by neurological disease. The diagnostic core is positive clinical evidence of incompatibility between the symptom and recognised neurological or medical conditions, for example Hoover's sign showing that 'paralysed' hip extension returns with contralateral movement. Option b states this incompatibility requirement, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Intentional production of symptoms is the hallmark of factitious disorder (for the sick role) or malingering (for external gain), not conversion disorder, where symptoms are not consciously feigned. The trap confuses conversion with deliberate fabrication.","c":"Concomitant substance use is not required and, if it fully explained the symptoms, would point away from a conversion diagnosis. It adds an irrelevant criterion.","d":"There is no requirement of onset before age 5; conversion disorder can present across the lifespan, often in adolescence or adulthood. The trap invents an arbitrary age cutoff."}},"m2_abn12":{"concept":"Dissociative identity disorder (DID) is characterised by a disruption of identity involving two or more distinct personality states (or an experience of possession), each with its own pattern of relating and perceiving. The diagnosis also requires recurrent gaps in the recall of everyday events, important personal information, or traumatic events beyond ordinary forgetting, plus significant distress or impairment, and it must not be a normal part of a broadly accepted cultural or religious practice or due to substances. Option c captures both the two-or-more identity states and the recurrent amnesia, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"DID is a psychological dissociative condition, not something defined by loss of consciousness on EEG; EEG changes belong to epilepsy or seizure disorders. The trap smuggles in a neurological criterion that does not apply.","b":"A single fugue state lasting under 24 hours describes dissociative fugue (a feature of dissociative amnesia), not the enduring multiple-identity structure of DID. It confuses fugue with DID.","d":"While trauma often precedes DID, the diagnosis does not require any specific precipitant such as sexual assault. The trap overstates one common etiology as a strict criterion."}},"m2_abn13":{"concept":"Anorexia nervosa in DSM-5 rests on three requirements: restriction of energy intake leading to significantly low body weight for age, sex, and health; an intense fear of gaining weight or persistent behaviour that interferes with weight gain even at low weight; and a disturbance in the way body weight or shape is experienced (or denial of the seriousness of low weight). Crucially DSM-5 removed the amenorrhoea criterion that DSM-IV had required. Option a lists exactly these three current criteria, so it is correct.","traps":{"b":"A BMI under 17 is not an absolute cutoff; 'significantly low weight' is judged in context, and BMI is used only to grade severity (mild ≥17, moderate 16 to 16.99, severe 15 to 15.99, extreme less than 15). The trap turns a severity marker into a rigid diagnostic threshold.","c":"Amenorrhoea was a DSM-IV criterion that DSM-5 deliberately dropped, partly because it could not apply to males or pre-menarchal patients. The trap rewards use of the outdated criterion.","d":"Purging occurs only in the binge-eating/purging subtype, not in all cases; the restricting subtype involves dieting and exercise without regular purging. It wrongly makes a subtype feature universal."}},"m2_abn14":{"concept":"Bulimia nervosa involves recurrent binge eating followed by inappropriate compensatory behaviours (self-induced vomiting, laxatives, fasting, excessive exercise), with self-evaluation unduly influenced by body shape and weight. DSM-5 lowered the frequency threshold from the DSM-IV requirement of twice per week to an average of at least once per week over three months. Option b states once per week for three months, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Twice per week for six months is the older DSM-IV frequency criterion, which DSM-5 relaxed. The trap rewards someone anchored to the pre-DSM-5 rule.","c":"Once per day for one month is far more frequent and briefer than any DSM criterion for bulimia. It is a fabricated threshold.","d":"Three times per week for one month does not match any edition's criteria and understates the required duration. It is another invented frequency."}},"m2_abn15":{"concept":"Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder is a neurodevelopmental disorder marked by inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity that interferes with functioning. DSM-5 raised the age-of-onset criterion so that several inattentive or hyperactive-impulsive symptoms need to have been present before age 12 (DSM-IV had required onset before age 7). Option a, age 12, is therefore correct.","traps":{"b":"Age 7 is the outdated DSM-IV onset cutoff that DSM-5 replaced with age 12. The trap rewards use of the old criterion.","c":"Age 6 matches no DSM edition's onset requirement; it is a distractor near the old cutoff. It plays on vague memory of an 'early childhood' threshold.","d":"Age 16 is too late and matches no criterion; symptoms must have begun well before this. It is an invented value."}},"m2_abn16":{"concept":"Antisocial personality disorder (ASPD) describes a pervasive pattern of disregard for and violation of others' rights. DSM-5 requires the individual to be at least 18 years old for the diagnosis, and there must be evidence of conduct disorder with onset before age 15. Before 18 a young person showing these features is diagnosed with conduct disorder, not ASPD. Option b correctly pairs 'at or after 18' with 'conduct disorder before age 15'.","traps":{"a":"Diagnosed before 16 is wrong because ASPD cannot be given under age 18, and 'CD before 12' misstates the conduct-disorder onset age of 15. It corrupts both numbers.","c":"Diagnosed before 18 is incorrect; the person must be at least 18. Although 'CD before 15' is right, the diagnostic age is wrong, so the whole option fails.","d":"Diagnosed only after 21 sets the age bar too high; DSM-5 uses 18, not 21. The trap inflates the minimum age."}},"m2_abn17":{"concept":"Borderline personality disorder (BPD) is a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image, and affect, plus marked impulsivity. DSM-5 lists nine criteria (fear of abandonment, unstable intense relationships, identity disturbance, impulsivity, recurrent suicidal or self-harm behaviour, affective instability, chronic emptiness, inappropriate intense anger, transient stress-related paranoia or dissociation), and a diagnosis requires at least five of these nine. Option c, five, is correct.","traps":{"a":"Three is below the required threshold; meeting only three criteria would not qualify for the diagnosis. The trap tempts anyone guessing a low cutoff.","b":"Four is still one short of the required five and matches no DSM rule. It is a near-miss distractor.","d":"Six exceeds the required minimum; only five of nine are needed. The trap rewards overestimating the cutoff."}},"m2_abn18":{"concept":"Folie à deux (French for 'madness of two') is a condition in which a delusional belief is transmitted from a person with an established psychotic disorder (the 'primary' or inducer) to one or more closely associated people who normally live in relative isolation with them. The classic French psychiatrists Charles Lasègue and Jean-Pierre Falret described it in 1877. In ICD-11 (and earlier ICD-10) the disorder is coded as <b>induced delusional disorder</b>, also historically called shared psychotic disorder, so option (d) is correct.","traps":{"a":"'Delusional disorder, with another individual' is not an ICD-11 category label; it blends the DSM-5 workaround with invented wording. ICD-11 keeps the distinct 'induced delusional disorder' entity rather than tagging it onto delusional disorder.","b":"Brief and limited psychotic disorder is a short-lived (less than about 3 months) psychosis in one person, defined by rapid onset and remission, not by a delusion shared through a close relationship. It exploits confusion between transient psychosis and induced/shared delusions.","c":"Schizotypal disorder is a persistent pattern of eccentric behaviour, odd beliefs, and social deficits on the schizophrenia spectrum, not a delusion 'caught' from another person. It plays on the word 'shared/odd beliefs' without the induction mechanism."}},"m2_abn19":{"concept":"In DSM-5, conduct disorder is a repetitive pattern of violating others' rights and major age-appropriate norms (aggression, destruction, deceit, serious rule-breaking). DSM-5 added the specifier <b>'with limited prosocial emotions'</b> to flag children showing callous-unemotional traits, which predict a more severe, stable course closer to adult antisocial personality disorder. It requires at least two of four features over 12 months: lack of remorse or guilt, callous-lack of empathy, being unconcerned about performance, and shallow or deficient affect, which is exactly option (c).","traps":{"a":"Hyperactivity and inattention are the core features of ADHD, a frequently comorbid but separate disorder. This trap swaps the callous-unemotional specifier for ADHD symptoms because the two often co-occur.","b":"Comorbid major depression can accompany conduct disorder but is not part of the limited-prosocial-emotions definition; the specifier is about deficient empathy and remorse, not mood. It exploits the idea that any comorbidity 'counts' toward a specifier.","d":"Onset before age 5 is not the criterion; DSM-5 instead distinguishes childhood-onset (before age 10) from adolescent-onset conduct disorder. The number is both wrong and belongs to the onset specifier, not the prosocial-emotions specifier."}},"m2_abn20":{"concept":"A 'cultural concept of distress' (DSM-5's updated term, replacing the older 'culture-bound syndrome') is a way that a particular cultural group experiences, expresses, and explains suffering; the pattern only makes full sense within that culture's meaning system. <b>Pibloktoq</b> (Arctic 'hysteria'), <b>brain fag</b> (West African students overwhelmed by study), and <b>dhat</b> (South Asian anxiety about semen loss) are textbook examples, making (d) correct. Others include koro, susto, ataque de nervios, and taijin kyofusho.","traps":{"a":"These are not DSM-5 anxiety disorder subtypes; DSM-5 anxiety disorders are categories like panic disorder, GAD, and specific phobia. The trap assumes any distress with a fear component must be a formal anxiety subtype.","b":"Substance-induced disorders are caused by intoxication or withdrawal from a drug, which is not the mechanism behind these culturally-patterned presentations. It exploits confusion between culturally shaped distress and pharmacological cause.","c":"ICD-11 dissociative disorders (e.g., dissociative amnesia, trance disorder) involve disruptions of consciousness, memory, or identity; although some cultural presentations include trance features, the three named here are not classified as ICD-11 dissociative disorders. The trap over-generalises the trance element of pibloktoq."}},"m2_abn21":{"concept":"A major depressive episode is diagnosed using nine symptoms, memorised by the mnemonic <b>SIG-E-CAPS</b> plus mood: depressed mood, anhedonia (loss of interest or pleasure), appetite or weight change, sleep disturbance, psychomotor agitation or retardation, fatigue, worthlessness or excessive guilt, poor concentration, and suicidality. DSM-5 requires <b>five or more</b> of these present nearly every day for at least two weeks, and crucially at least one of the five must be depressed mood or anhedonia, so (c) is correct.","traps":{"a":"Three symptoms is below threshold and would capture many people with subthreshold or mild low mood who do not meet episode criteria. It exploits guessing a smaller number and forgetting the mood/anhedonia gateway rule.","b":"Four symptoms is still one short of the DSM-5 cut-off of five; a common near-miss error. Four symptoms describes subsyndromal depression, not a full episode.","d":"Requiring all nine would make the diagnosis nearly impossible and is far stricter than any manual specifies. This trap confuses 'at least five' with 'all of them'."}},"m2_abn22":{"concept":"Body dysmorphic disorder (BDD) is a preoccupation with one or more perceived defects in physical appearance that are unobservable or appear only slight to others, accompanied by repetitive behaviours (mirror-checking, grooming, reassurance-seeking) or mental acts (comparing). Because the preoccupation and repetitive behaviours resemble obsessions and compulsions, DSM-5 moved BDD into the new <b>Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders</b> chapter alongside OCD, hoarding disorder, trichotillomania, and excoriation disorder, so (d) is correct.","traps":{"a":"Somatic symptom and related disorders center on distress about actual physical symptoms and health, not on a perceived cosmetic defect. This is the classic DSM-IV-style trap, since BDD was historically grouped near the somatoform disorders before DSM-5 reclassified it.","b":"Personality disorders are pervasive, enduring patterns of inner experience and behaviour, not a circumscribed appearance preoccupation. It plays on the chronic self-focus of BDD being mistaken for a personality-level trait.","c":"Anxiety disorders are defined by excessive fear and anxiety, and although BDD involves anxiety, DSM-5 specifically placed it with OCD-related disorders because of the obsession-compulsion structure. The trap exploits the anxious surface of the disorder."}},"m2_abn23":{"concept":"Narcissistic personality disorder (NPD) is a pervasive pattern of grandiosity, need for admiration, and lack of empathy beginning by early adulthood, with at least five of nine DSM-5 criteria. The core triad is best summarised as <b>grandiosity, need for admiration, and lack of empathy</b>, which is option (a); these three threads run through the individual criteria (grandiose self-importance, fantasies of success, sense of specialness, entitlement, exploitativeness, envy, arrogance).","traps":{"b":"Impulsivity, identity disturbance, and anger describe borderline personality disorder, whose hallmark is instability of affect, relationships, and self-image. The trap swaps one Cluster B disorder for another.","c":"Suspiciousness, social anxiety, and eccentricity point to Cluster A (paranoid and schizotypal personality disorders), not narcissism. It exploits mixing up the odd/eccentric cluster with the dramatic cluster.","d":"Submissiveness, fear of separation, and reassurance-seeking describe dependent personality disorder, a Cluster C anxious presentation that is essentially the opposite of narcissistic self-focus. The trap tests whether you can tell entitlement from clinging dependence."}},"m2_abn24":{"concept":"La belle indifférence is a striking, apparently calm lack of concern about a serious neurological symptom (e.g., a paralysed limb or blindness). It was classically linked to <b>conversion disorder</b> (functional neurological symptom disorder), where neurological symptoms are incompatible with recognised disease, so (d) is the intended answer. Note that DSM-5-TR warns the sign is neither sensitive nor specific and must not be used to confirm the diagnosis.","traps":{"a":"Malingering is the conscious fabrication of symptoms for a clear external gain; a malingerer usually emphasises rather than seems indifferent to symptoms, and it is not a mental disorder. The trap tests confusing feigning for gain with the unconscious conversion presentation.","b":"Somatic symptom disorder involves excessive thoughts, feelings, and anxiety ABOUT physical symptoms, which is the opposite of indifference. It exploits the shared 'bodily complaint' surface while ignoring that the emotional tone is inverted.","c":"Factitious disorder involves intentionally producing symptoms to occupy the sick role, without the classic serene indifference to a genuine functional deficit. The trap groups all the somatic-related disorders together and picks the wrong one."}},"m2_abn25":{"concept":"Factitious disorder involves deliberately falsifying physical or psychological signs or inducing injury or disease, but crucially <b>without any obvious external reward</b>; the drive is internal, to occupy the sick role (or, in the imposed-on-another/Munchausen-by-proxy form, to have a dependent be seen as ill). Malingering shares the intentional faking but is done <b>for an external incentive</b> such as money, drugs, or avoiding duty, and is not a mental disorder. The distinguishing feature is therefore the absence of obvious external incentives, option (d).","traps":{"a":"Presence of dissociation is not what separates factitious disorder from malingering; both involve deliberate, conscious deception, not dissociative gaps in awareness. It exploits confusing intentional feigning with dissociative phenomena.","b":"Comorbid substance use can occur in either condition and is not a defining distinction. The trap offers a plausible-sounding clinical feature that has no bearing on the core motivational difference.","c":"Onset only in adolescence is false; factitious disorder typically emerges in early adulthood and has no adolescence-restricted rule. The trap invents a developmental criterion to distract from the real motivation-based distinction."}},"m2_abn26":{"concept":"DSM-5 abolished the old bipolar 'mixed episode' (which needed full mania AND full depression at once) and replaced it with the flexible <b>'with mixed features' specifier</b>. When someone in a manic or hypomanic episode also shows several symptoms of the opposite pole, the specifier requires concurrent <b>depressive</b> symptoms on most days, so (c) is correct. This captures far more real patients than the rigid DSM-IV threshold did.","traps":{"a":"Psychosis is coded by the separate 'with psychotic features' specifier (mood-congruent or incongruent), not by mixed features. The trap swaps one mood-episode specifier for another.","b":"Anxiety corresponds to the distinct 'with anxious distress' specifier, which is a different add-on entirely. It exploits the fact that DSM-5 has many specifiers by picking a real one that answers a different question.","d":"Substance withdrawal points toward a substance-induced mood disorder, an alternative diagnosis rather than a feature of a primary mixed presentation. The trap confuses aetiology (drug cause) with the mixed-features symptom pattern."}},"m2_abn27":{"concept":"Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD) was introduced in DSM-5 to reduce over-diagnosis of paediatric bipolar disorder in chronically irritable children. It is defined by severe recurrent temper outbursts (verbal or behavioural) grossly out of proportion to the situation, at least three times per week, with a persistently irritable or angry mood between outbursts, present for 12 or more months. Onset must be <b>before age 10</b>, and the diagnosis is only made between ages 6 and 18, so (d) is correct.","traps":{"a":"Age 6 is the lower bound at which the diagnosis may first be MADE, not the required onset age; symptoms must have begun before 10, and both are needed. The trap confuses the diagnosable-age floor with the onset criterion.","b":"Age 16 is wrong and inverts the rule; DMDD must not be first diagnosed after age 18 and onset is early, not mid-adolescence. It exploits guessing a late number.","c":"Age 12 is a common distractor because 12 marks other developmental thresholds, but the DMDD onset cut-off is specifically before 10. The trap banks on a near-miss numerical error."}},"m2_abn28":{"concept":"Anorexia nervosa is characterised by restriction of energy intake leading to significantly low body weight, intense fear of weight gain, and disturbed body image. Chronic starvation produces a recognisable medical picture: <b>lanugo</b> (fine downy body hair), <b>bradycardia</b> and hypotension, hypothermia, <b>amenorrhoea</b>, and electrolyte disturbance including <b>hypokalaemia</b> (worse with purging), so option (b) lists the correct findings. The body enters a low-metabolic, euthyroid-sick state (low T3), and rapid refeeding risks dangerous refeeding syndrome.","traps":{"a":"Hyperthyroidism is the opposite of the metabolic picture in anorexia, where starvation lowers thyroid activity (low T3, euthyroid sick). The trap exploits assuming weight loss means an overactive thyroid.","c":"Polyphagia (excessive hunger and eating) contradicts the restrictive core of anorexia nervosa; it is seen in conditions like diabetes or hyperthyroidism. It plays on reversing the eating behaviour.","d":"Hyperkalaemia and tachycardia are the wrong direction; anorexia typically produces hypokalaemia and bradycardia. The trap inverts both electrolyte and heart-rate findings to catch careless reading."}},"m2_abn29":{"concept":"A specific phobia is marked, excessive fear or anxiety about a particular object or situation (e.g., heights, animals, injections) that is almost always triggered by it and actively avoided. DSM-5 standardised the duration requirement for most adult anxiety disorders to <b>at least 6 months</b>, including specific phobia, social anxiety disorder, agoraphobia, GAD, and separation anxiety disorder, so (a) is correct. This change reduced over-diagnosis of transient fears.","traps":{"b":"One month is the duration tied to panic disorder (persistent worry about further attacks for a month) and to some trauma/acute presentations, not to specific phobia. The trap swaps in the wrong anxiety-disorder time frame.","c":"Three months is not a DSM-5 specific-phobia criterion; it appears in other contexts (e.g., adjustment disorder resolution), so it is a plausible-looking distractor with no basis here. It exploits fuzzy memory of assorted DSM durations.","d":"One week is far too short and would capture ordinary passing fears that DSM-5 deliberately excludes with the 6-month rule. The trap offers the shortest option to catch guessing."}},"m2_abn30":{"concept":"Schizoaffective disorder is a diagnosis for someone who has features of both a psychotic disorder and a mood disorder, and DSM-5 sets strict rules so it is not overused. There must be an uninterrupted period of illness during which a major mood episode (major depressive or manic) occurs at the same time as Criterion A of schizophrenia (delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech, etc.). Critically, the person must also have had delusions or hallucinations for at least 2 weeks in the ABSENCE of a prominent mood episode at some point in the lifetime, and mood symptoms must be present for the majority of the total illness. Option a captures both defining pillars: mood episode overlapping active psychosis PLUS ≥2 weeks of pure psychosis without prominent mood symptoms.","traps":{"b":"If mood symptoms occur ONLY during acute psychosis (and never a stand-alone psychotic stretch), the picture fits schizophrenia (where brief mood symptoms are common) or a mood disorder with psychotic features, not schizoaffective disorder. This is the exact opposite of the ≥2-weeks-of-psychosis-without-mood requirement.","c":"Lifetime absence of psychosis rules out any psychotic disorder entirely; schizoaffective disorder by definition requires psychosis meeting schizophrenia Criterion A. This is a distractor that contradicts the core of the diagnosis.","d":"Bipolar features alone describe bipolar disorder. Schizoaffective disorder is subtyped as bipolar type or depressive type, but the mood component is only one half; the independent psychosis requirement is what the stem tests, so 'bipolar features only' is incomplete and wrong."}},"m2_ass01":{"concept":"The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition (WAIS-IV, David Wechsler's test, 2008 revision) organises subtests into four index scores that together produce the Full Scale IQ. Those four indices are Verbal Comprehension (VCI), Perceptual Reasoning (PRI), Working Memory (WMI), and Processing Speed (PSI). WAIS-IV deliberately abandoned the older Verbal IQ / Performance IQ split used in WAIS-III and earlier, moving to this four-factor structure, which is why option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"Verbal IQ, Performance IQ and Full Scale IQ belong to the OLDER Wechsler model (WAIS-III and before); 'Memory Quotient' is from the Wechsler Memory Scale, a separate instrument. This is the pre-2008 dichotomy the WAIS-IV explicitly replaced.","b":"Crystallised and fluid intelligence come from Raymond Cattell and John Horn's Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) theory, not the WAIS-IV index labels. 'Visual IQ' and 'Auditory IQ' are not Wechsler indices at all; this option invents terms.","d":"Cognitive, behavioural, emotional and adaptive are broad domains of functioning assessed by various scales (e.g., adaptive behaviour measures), not the four WAIS-IV cognitive indices. It confuses domains of assessment with a specific IQ test's structure."}},"m2_ass02":{"concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI, originally by Starke Hathaway and John McKinley) includes validity scales that check HOW someone answered, not just what they endorsed. The L (Lie) scale is made of items describing minor faults that almost everyone honestly admits (e.g., occasionally getting angry). Someone naively trying to look virtuous ('fake good') denies these common human flaws, which raises L. So the L scale detects unsophisticated impression-management, making option b correct.","traps":{"a":"Random or inconsistent responding is captured by VRIN (Variable Response Inconsistency) and TRIN (True Response Inconsistency), not the L scale. This confuses a defensiveness scale with the inconsistency scales.","c":"Severe psychopathology is indexed by the F (Infrequency) scale and the clinical scales, not L. In fact a high L suggests defensiveness that may HIDE pathology, the opposite of detecting it.","d":"Reading-level limitations affect overall validity and can inflate inconsistency indices, but the L scale is not a reading measure. This is an unrelated distractor about test administration requirements."}},"m2_ass03":{"concept":"The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales (originally the Vineland Social Maturity Scale by Edgar Doll, 1935; now Vineland-3) measure adaptive behaviour, meaning how well a person copes with everyday demands independently, gathered via interview with a caregiver. Its core domains are Communication, Daily Living Skills, Socialisation, and Motor Skills (mainly for young children), with an optional Maladaptive Behavior index. Because DSM-5 diagnosis of intellectual disability requires BOTH low IQ and impaired adaptive functioning, the Vineland supplies the adaptive-functioning side, making option a correct.","traps":{"b":"Personality traits are measured by instruments like the MMPI, 16PF or NEO-PI-R, not the Vineland. This confuses adaptive behaviour (what a person can do day to day) with personality structure.","c":"IQ is measured by cognitive tests such as the Wechsler or Stanford-Binet scales. The Vineland deliberately measures adaptive behaviour as a SEPARATE construct that complements IQ in diagnosing intellectual disability.","d":"Severity of psychopathology is assessed by clinical/symptom scales, not the Vineland. The trap is assuming any clinical scale rates illness severity, when the Vineland instead rates everyday functional competence."}},"m2_ass04":{"concept":"Wechsler IQ scores are standardised with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation (SD) of 15, and they follow a normal distribution. An IQ of 85 is exactly one SD below the mean (100 − 15 = 85), which corresponds to the 16th percentile because about 34% of cases sit between the mean and −1 SD, leaving roughly 16% below −1 SD. Hence option c (16th percentile) is correct.","traps":{"a":"The 10th percentile corresponds to roughly IQ 81 (about −1.28 SD), not IQ 85. This is a near-miss distractor for anyone who guesses without computing the SD position.","b":"The 25th percentile is about IQ 90 (roughly −0.67 SD). It is close enough to tempt a rough guesser but does not match the exact −1 SD point.","d":"The 50th percentile is the mean itself, IQ 100, not 85. This trap catches anyone who forgets that 85 is a full SD below average rather than average."}},"m2_ass05":{"concept":"The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is a projective test in which the person tells stories about ambiguous interpersonal pictures, on the assumption that they 'project' their own needs and conflicts into the stories. It was developed in 1935 by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan at the Harvard Psychological Clinic, grounded in Murray's need-press theory of personality. Therefore option d names the correct authors.","traps":{"a":"Hermann Rorschach created the Rorschach Inkblot Test (1921), a different projective method using inkblots rather than pictured scenes. Classic trap of swapping one famous projective-test author for another.","b":"John Exner developed the Comprehensive System for SCORING the Rorschach, not the TAT. He standardised interpretation of an existing test rather than authoring the TAT.","c":"Leopold Bellak adapted the idea into the Children's Apperception Test (CAT), using animal pictures for children. He extended the TAT concept but did not create the original TAT."}},"m2_ass06":{"concept":"In a normal (bell-shaped) distribution, cases cluster symmetrically around the mean, and the empirical '68-95-99.7 rule' describes how much of the data falls within successive standard deviations (SD). Within ±1 SD lies about 68%, within ±2 SD about 95%, and within ±3 SD about 99.7%. The stem asks for ±2 SD, so option c (~95%) is correct.","traps":{"a":"~50% corresponds roughly to the interquartile range (about ±0.67 SD), not ±2 SD. This trap tempts anyone who confuses 'half the cases' with a two-SD span.","b":"~68% is the proportion within ±1 SD, not ±2 SD. Classic off-by-one-SD error in the empirical rule.","d":"~99.7% is the proportion within ±3 SD, not ±2 SD. It over-shoots by one standard deviation."}},"m2_ass07":{"concept":"Reliability refers to the consistency of a measurement. Cronbach's alpha (developed by Lee Cronbach, 1951) is a coefficient that estimates internal consistency: it is essentially the average of all possible split-half correlations, telling you how well the items on a scale hang together as measures of the same construct. Because it is computed from a single administration and reflects item interrelatedness, option a (internal consistency reliability) is correct.","traps":{"b":"Test-retest reliability requires administering the SAME test twice at different times and correlating the scores; alpha uses only one administration. This confuses stability over time with internal item consistency.","c":"Construct validity concerns whether a test measures the intended theoretical construct, shown through convergent and discriminant evidence; it is validity, not reliability. Alpha is a reliability index, and reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity.","d":"Inter-rater reliability is the agreement between different raters (Cohen's kappa for categorical, intraclass correlation for continuous). It involves multiple judges, whereas alpha involves multiple items within one test."}},"m2_ass08":{"concept":"Criterion validity asks whether a test correlates with an external benchmark (criterion) that reflects the thing you care about. It has two forms based on timing: predictive validity correlates the test with a criterion measured LATER (e.g., an entrance exam predicting future first-year grades), whereas concurrent validity uses a criterion measured at the same time. Because the stem specifies a future criterion, option b is the correct definition of predictive validity.","traps":{"a":"Asking experts whether items 'look right' describes face validity, the most superficial form, based on appearance rather than any statistical link to a criterion. It is not predictive validity.","c":"Splitting the test in half and correlating the halves is split-half reliability, an internal-consistency estimate, not a validity procedure at all. This trap swaps a reliability method for a validity one.","d":"Comparing the test to itself two weeks later is test-retest reliability, measuring stability over time, not the relationship to an external future criterion. Again reliability, not predictive validity."}},"m2_ass09":{"concept":"T-scores are standardised scores with a mean of 50 and a standard deviation (SD) of 10, used on the MMPI-2. To convert a T-score to SD units, subtract the mean and divide by the SD: (65 − 50) / 10 = 1.5, so T=65 sits 1.5 SD above the mean (about the 92nd percentile). Option d (+1.5 SD) is therefore correct, and this cutoff is why T≥65 is treated as clinically significant on the MMPI-2.","traps":{"a":"+0.5 SD would be T=55, not T=65. This trap catches anyone who miscomputes the distance between the score and the mean.","b":"+1.0 SD equals T=60. It is a common wrong answer for those who round 15 points down to one SD instead of one-and-a-half.","c":"+2.0 SD equals T=70, which was the cutoff on the ORIGINAL MMPI. The MMPI-2 lowered the threshold to T=65 (+1.5 SD), so this option confuses the old cutoff with the revised one."}},"m2_ass10":{"concept":"The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test (Lauretta Bender, 1938; second edition BG-II, 2003) asks the person to copy a series of geometric Gestalt designs. Performance is scored for how accurately the visual figure is translated into a motor drawing, so it primarily assesses visual-motor integration and, historically, screens for neurological or brain dysfunction that disrupts that integration. Option d correctly identifies this purpose.","traps":{"a":"Personality structure is assessed by projective or self-report personality tests; although the Bender has occasionally been used projectively, its PRIMARY, validated use is visual-motor screening, so this is not its main purpose.","b":"Severity of depression is measured by symptom scales such as the Beck Depression Inventory or Hamilton scale, not by copying geometric figures. This is an unrelated clinical distractor.","c":"Reading comprehension is an academic-achievement skill measured by educational tests; the Bender involves copying designs, not reading. The trap assumes any paper-and-pencil task taps literacy."}},"m2_ass11":{"concept":"The Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children, Fifth Edition (WISC-V, 2014) is the child version of the Wechsler series and is normed for ages 6 years 0 months through 16 years 11 months. Below that, the WPPSI-IV covers 2:6–7:7 (preschool), and above it the WAIS-IV covers 16:0–90:11 (adults). This age banding makes option d correct.","traps":{"a":"2:6–7:7 is the range of the WPPSI-IV (Wechsler Preschool and Primary Scale of Intelligence), the preschool test, not the WISC-V. This trap swaps the child scale for the preschool scale.","b":"0–42 months (roughly 1–42 months) is the range of infant developmental measures such as the Bayley Scales, not a Wechsler intelligence test for school-age children.","c":"16:0–90:11 is the WAIS-IV adult range. It is adjacent to the WISC-V ceiling, which is exactly why it is a tempting distractor, but the WISC-V is for children, ending at 16:11."}},"m2_ass12":{"concept":"The standard error of measurement (SEM) expresses how much an individual's observed test score is expected to fluctuate around their true score due to measurement error. It is derived from classical test theory, where the observed score equals the true score plus error. The formula is SEM = SD × √(1 − r_xx), where SD is the standard deviation of the test and r_xx is the reliability coefficient. Option c gives exactly this: as reliability approaches 1 the error term √(1 − r_xx) shrinks toward 0, and as reliability drops the SEM widens, which is why SEM is used to build confidence bands (±1 SEM ≈ 68 percent, ±1.96 SEM ≈ 95 percent) around an observed score.","traps":{"a":"SD × √reliability is the formula for the true-score standard deviation, not the error. It multiplies by reliability rather than its complement, so it grows as reliability rises, which is the opposite of how measurement error behaves. The trap is swapping r_xx for (1 − r_xx) inside the root.","b":"Reliability × validity is not a defined psychometric quantity and produces a unitless product, not an error expressed in score units. It baits the reader who remembers that reliability and validity are linked but forgets the actual SEM formula.","d":"Mean − SD is an arbitrary arithmetic combination with no basis in measurement theory; it would even change sign depending on the numbers. It is a distractor for someone guessing from familiar statistical symbols."}},"m2_ass13":{"concept":"The 16PF (Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire) is a trait-based personality inventory built by applying factor analysis to the language of personality. Raymond Cattell started from Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert's lexical list of trait words, reduced them through his L-data (life records), Q-data (questionnaire), and T-data (objective test) approach, and extracted 16 source traits, which is why option b is correct. Cattell distinguished these underlying source traits from the more visible surface traits.","traps":{"a":"Hans Eysenck built the PEN model and the EPQ, reducing personality to three higher-order dimensions (Psychoticism, Extraversion, Neuroticism), far fewer than Cattell's 16. The trap exploits that both men were factor-analytic trait theorists.","c":"Paul Costa and Robert McCrae developed the NEO-PI-R measuring the Big Five, not the 16PF. They are a later generation and their five-domain model is often contrasted with Cattell's sixteen factors.","d":"Gordon Allport supplied the lexical trait words and the cardinal/central/secondary trait scheme, but he was skeptical of factor analysis and did not build the 16PF. The trap rewards knowing he was foundational to trait theory while missing that he did not construct this test."}},"m2_ass14":{"concept":"In classical test theory a test cannot correlate with an outside criterion more strongly than it correlates with itself, because unreliable (noisy) measurement attenuates every correlation. The general ceiling on a validity coefficient is √(r_xx × r_yy), the product of the square roots of the two reliabilities. When the criterion is measured perfectly (r_yy = 1) this reduces to √r_xx, so option a is correct; for example a test with reliability .64 can validly correlate at most √.64 = .80 with a flawless criterion.","traps":{"b":"Squaring the reliability (r_xx²) makes the ceiling smaller than the reliability itself, which is backward, because the true relationship involves taking the square root, not the square. The trap inverts the mathematical operation.","c":"Setting maximum validity equal to reliability itself ignores that the square-root relationship makes the ceiling higher than r_xx (since reliabilities are between 0 and 1). It is the near-miss for someone who recalls that reliability limits validity but not the exact form.","d":"Reliability minus SEM subtracts an error term measured in raw score units from a unitless coefficient, mixing incompatible quantities. It is a nonsense combination that baits recognition of two familiar terms."}},"m2_ass15":{"concept":"The Rorschach inkblot test spawned several competing American scoring systems in the mid-20th century, each with different codes and norms, which made research hard to compare. John Exner reviewed the five major systems (those of Samuel Beck, Bruno Klopfer, Marguerite Hertz, Zygmunt Piotrowski, and David Rapaport with Roy Schafer), kept only the empirically defensible components, and in 1974 published the integrated Comprehensive System, making option b correct. It became the dominant approach and was later succeeded by R-PAS (Meyer and colleagues, 2011).","traps":{"a":"Samuel Beck authored one of the five earlier American Rorschach systems that Exner drew upon, but he did not create the integrated Comprehensive System. The trap offers a real Rorschach figure who came before the synthesis.","c":"Bruno Klopfer developed another of the pre-Exner scoring systems, known for a more phenomenological, experience-based approach. He is a legitimate contributor but not the author of the Comprehensive System.","d":"Zygmunt Piotrowski created the Perceptanalysis system, again one of the sources Exner consolidated rather than the consolidator. All three wrong options bait recognition of genuine Rorschach names."}},"m2_ass16":{"concept":"Criterion-related validity asks whether test scores relate to an external standard (the criterion), and it splits by the timing of that criterion. Concurrent validity is established when the test and the criterion are gathered at essentially the same time, for instance validating a new depression scale against a clinician's HAM-D rating taken the same day, which is exactly option b. Predictive validity, by contrast, uses a criterion collected in the future (e.g., a GRE score predicting graduate GPA years later).","traps":{"a":"Measuring the criterion before the test describes a retrospective or postdictive design, not concurrent validity, and is not the standard textbook contrast. The trap plays on the reader knowing that timing matters but picking the wrong temporal order.","c":"Claiming concurrent validity cannot be quantified is false; it is expressed as a correlation coefficient between test and criterion just like predictive validity. It exploits vague discomfort with validity being harder to pin down than reliability.","d":"Internal consistency (e.g., Cronbach's alpha) is a form of reliability describing item interrelatedness, not a criterion-related validity at all. The trap confuses a reliability index with a validity type."}},"m2_ass17":{"concept":"The MMPI includes validity scales that gauge how a person approached the test, separate from the clinical symptom scales. The K scale detects subtle defensiveness or a guarded, image-protecting test-taking attitude, and its most distinctive use is as a statistical correction: fractions of the K raw score are added back to certain clinical scales (1, 4, 7, 8, and 9) to compensate for the underreporting such defensiveness causes, which makes option b correct. This K-correction sharpens those scales for people who minimize their problems.","traps":{"a":"Flagging random responding is the job of the F and VRIN scales, not K. The trap swaps one validity scale's function for another's.","c":"Measuring depression is the role of clinical Scale 2, a symptom scale, whereas K is a validity/response-style scale. It baits confusion between validity and clinical scales.","d":"Detecting inconsistent responding is done by VRIN (variable response inconsistency) and TRIN (true response inconsistency), not K. The trap offers a real MMPI validity function attached to the wrong scale."}},"m2_ass18":{"concept":"Intellectual disability is not diagnosed from an IQ number alone. DSM-5 requires deficits in BOTH intellectual functioning (reasoning, problem solving, roughly two standard deviations below the mean, an IQ around 65 to 75 once the standard error of measurement is allowed for) AND adaptive functioning across conceptual, social, and practical domains, with onset in the developmental period. A WAIS-IV FSIQ of 70 sits in the borderline region, so without documented adaptive deficits the correct description is borderline intellectual functioning, making option b right; DSM-5 also sets severity by adaptive functioning rather than by IQ bands.","traps":{"a":"Average functioning corresponds to an IQ near 90 to 109, far above 70, so labeling a 70 as average is simply the wrong band. It baits a careless reading of the score.","c":"Calling it mild intellectual disability solely on the score violates the DSM-5 requirement that adaptive deficits also be present; a score of 70 alone is insufficient. This is the central trap the question tests.","d":"Profound intellectual disability involves extremely limited functioning with IQ far below 70 (roughly below 20 to 25 in older systems) and pervasive adaptive deficits, nowhere near a score of 70. It is an extreme distractor."}},"m2_ass19":{"concept":"Henry Murray's personology framed behavior as the interaction between internal needs (motives such as achievement, affiliation, and aggression) and environmental press (the forces a situation exerts on the person). To tap these he and Christiana Morgan built the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), where the client makes up stories about ambiguous pictures and the examiner reads off the protagonist's needs and the press acting on them, so option c is correct. The need-press system is the direct conceptual engine of TAT scoring.","traps":{"a":"The Rorschach is scored for perceptual organization and determinants (form, color, movement), not for Murray's needs and press. The trap offers the most famous projective test as a decoy.","b":"Sentence Completion Tests such as Julian Rotter's Incomplete Sentences Blank elicit attitudes and conflicts from stems, not need-press dynamics. It baits familiarity with another projective format.","d":"The Word Association Test descends from Carl Jung's complex-indicator work on reaction times and disturbed responses, unrelated to Murray. The trap rewards recognizing a projective method while missing its theoretical parent."}},"m2_ass20":{"concept":"The Beck Depression Inventory-II is a self-report measure of depression severity revised by Aaron Beck and colleagues in 1996 to align with DSM-IV criteria. It contains 21 items, each presenting a graded set of statements scored 0 to 3, and the respondent rates how they have felt over the past two weeks, giving a total range of 0 to 63; this exactly matches option a. Standard cutoffs are 0 to 13 minimal, 14 to 19 mild, 20 to 28 moderate, and 29 to 63 severe.","traps":{"b":"31 items rated 0 to 4 does not match any BDI-II specification; both the item count and the rating range are wrong. The trap supplies plausible-looking but incorrect numbers.","c":"10 items rated 0 to 10 describes neither the BDI-II nor a standard depression scale of this form; the 0 to 10 anchoring resembles a single visual-analog rating, not the BDI item structure. It baits guessing from round numbers.","d":"A single item rated 0 to 100 is a visual analog or global severity slider, not a multi-symptom inventory like the BDI-II, and could not cover the range of depressive symptoms. It is an obvious oversimplification distractor."}},"m2_ass21":{"concept":"Standard scores rescale raw scores onto a chosen mean and standard deviation so results can be compared across tests. The scaled score used for the individual subtests of the Wechsler scales (WAIS and WISC) is fixed at a mean of 10 and a standard deviation of 3, running from 1 to 19, which is precisely option b. This lets a clinician compare a person's subtest performance to the normative average of 10.","traps":{"a":"A Z score has a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 1, not 10 and 3. The trap tests whether the reader knows each scale's specific parameters.","c":"A T score has a mean of 50 and a standard deviation of 10, used for scales like the MMPI, not the M=10, SD=3 metric asked about. It baits confusing two commonly taught standard scores.","d":"A stanine has a mean of about 5 and a standard deviation of roughly 2, spanning 1 to 9. Its parameters differ from the Wechsler subtest metric, so it is the wrong standardized scale."}},"m2_ass22":{"concept":"The NEO-PI-R is the flagship measure of the Big Five (Five-Factor Model) of personality, developed by Paul Costa and Robert McCrae in 1992. It assesses five broad domains, Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism, and crucially subdivides each domain into 6 more specific facets, giving 30 facets across 240 items, which matches option c. The shorter NEO-FFI (60 items) measures only the five domains without the facets.","traps":{"a":"Three dimensions labeled PEN is Hans Eysenck's model measured by the EPQ, not the NEO-PI-R. The trap swaps one trait theorist's structure for another's.","b":"16 source traits belong to Raymond Cattell's 16PF, a different inventory. It baits confusion among the major factor-analytic personality models.","d":"11 personality disorder scales suggests a clinical instrument such as the MCMI, not the normal-range Five-Factor NEO-PI-R. The trap mixes a normal-personality measure with a disorder-oriented one."}},"m2_ass23":{"concept":"The Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Y-BOCS), developed by Wayne Goodman and colleagues in 1989, is the standard clinician-rated measure of obsessive-compulsive disorder severity. Its 10 items rate five dimensions, time occupied, interference, distress, resistance, and degree of control, separately for obsessions and compulsions, yielding a 0 to 40 total with cutoffs commonly set near 8, 16, 24, and 32; this is why option b is correct. Importantly it gauges severity independent of the specific symptom content.","traps":{"a":"Schizophrenia symptom severity is assessed by scales such as the PANSS or BPRS, not the Y-BOCS. The trap offers a different disorder with its own dedicated instruments.","c":"Depression severity is captured by the Beck Depression Inventory or the Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression, not by the Y-BOCS. It baits confusing named clinical scales across disorders.","d":"PTSD severity is measured by instruments like the CAPS or PCL, not the Y-BOCS. The trap rewards recognizing a symptom-severity scale while attaching it to the wrong diagnosis."}},"m2_ass24":{"concept":"Wechsler subtest scaled scores are standardized to a mean of 10 and a standard deviation of 3, so each 3-point step equals one SD. A scaled score of 7 is exactly 1 SD below the mean (10 minus 3), placing it at roughly the 16th percentile, which is still inside the normal low-average-to-average band, not an impairment range. Option d is correct because it names this position accurately (1 SD below mean, within average range).","traps":{"a":"Profound impairment on Wechsler subtests would require a scaled score at the very floor (around 1, more than 2.5 SD below the mean); 7 is only 1 SD down, nowhere near impaired. Confuses a mildly-below-average score with a severe deficit.","b":"Significantly above average would be a scaled score around 13 or higher (1 SD or more above the mean of 10); a 7 is below the mean, not above it. Reverses the direction of the deviation.","c":"Borderline impairment corresponds to about 2 SD below the mean, i.e. a scaled score near 4, not 7. Mistakes a 1-SD dip for the 2-SD borderline zone."}},"m2_ass25":{"concept":"Test-retest reliability estimates consistency by giving the same test to the same people twice and correlating the two sets of scores; it assumes the thing being measured has not genuinely changed between administrations. That assumption only holds for stable constructs, so it is most appropriate for trait measures such as personality dimensions or intelligence, where the true value should be steady over time (option b).","traps":{"a":"A rapidly changing state (like current mood or anxiety at this moment) is expected to differ between two testings, so a low retest correlation reflects real change, not unreliability. This inverts the logic: states are the worst case for test-retest.","c":"Number of items per scale is irrelevant to whether test-retest applies; single-item measures can still be re-administered. Confuses test-retest with internal-consistency methods (like Cronbach's alpha or split-half) that actually require multiple items.","d":"Test-retest is not restricted to open-ended interviews; it applies to any standardized measure re-administered over time. Arbitrarily ties a general reliability method to one narrow format."}},"m2_the01":{"concept":"Aaron Beck's cognitive model holds that depression is maintained by a pattern of automatic negative thoughts he called the cognitive triad: systematically negative appraisals of the self, of the world or one's ongoing experience, and of the future. Option d names these three domains exactly, and they are the core target of cognitive restructuring in CBT.","traps":{"a":"Id, ego and superego are Sigmund Freud's three psychoanalytic structures of the mind, not cognitive appraisals. Imports a psychodynamic model where a cognitive one is asked for.","b":"Mother, father and self evoke object-relations or attachment thinking, not Beck's triad. A plausible-sounding trio that has nothing to do with the cognitive model of depression.","c":"Past, present and future is a tempting near-miss because the future is genuinely one leg of the triad, but the actual triad is self, world and future, not the three tenses of time. Swaps two real components (self and world) for a false symmetric-sounding set."}},"m2_the02":{"concept":"The empty-chair technique places an imagined person or a disowned part of the self in a physically empty chair so the client can address it directly and then, often, switch chairs to voice the other side. It was developed by Fritz Perls within Gestalt therapy to bring unfinished business and split-off feelings into present-moment awareness and promote integration (option a).","traps":{"b":"CBT works by identifying and testing distorted cognitions through methods like thought records and behavioural experiments, not by role-played chair dialogues. Attaches an experiential Gestalt method to a cognitive-behavioural school.","c":"Person-centred therapy (Carl Rogers) relies on the therapeutic relationship and core conditions rather than structured techniques like chair work. Confuses two different humanistic therapies that both value experience but use very different methods.","d":"Psychoanalysis uses free association, dream interpretation and transference, not an empty chair. Mislabels an active, present-focused technique as a classical analytic one."}},"m2_the03":{"concept":"Carl Rogers argued that therapeutic change does not depend on techniques but on the quality of the relationship the therapist offers. In his 1957 statement of the necessary and sufficient conditions, the three therapist-provided core conditions are unconditional positive regard (nonjudgmental acceptance), congruence (genuineness/transparency), and accurate empathic understanding, which the client must also perceive (option c).","traps":{"a":"Transference, free association and interpretation are the working tools of psychoanalysis, not Rogers's humanistic conditions. Substitutes a psychodynamic toolkit for the person-centred one.","b":"Cognitive restructuring, behavioural activation and exposure are core CBT techniques, again technique-driven rather than relationship-driven. Confuses the mechanism-focused CBT model with Rogers's relational conditions.","d":"Working alliance, transference and resistance interpretation are psychodynamic concepts; while 'alliance' overlaps with modern common-factors research, this trio is not Rogers's three core conditions. Plays on the word 'alliance' sounding relational."}},"m2_the04":{"concept":"Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe in 1958, treats anxiety by pairing a feared stimulus with an incompatible relaxed state, so relaxation reciprocally inhibits the anxiety response. The procedure teaches deep relaxation (often progressive muscle relaxation), builds a graded hierarchy of feared situations, and then moves up that hierarchy in imagination or in vivo while the client stays relaxed (option c).","traps":{"a":"Aversive conditioning pairs an unwanted behaviour with an unpleasant stimulus to reduce it; it is essentially the opposite goal of relaxation-based desensitisation. Confuses a different behavioural technique that also involves conditioning.","b":"Flooding exposes the client to the most feared stimulus at full intensity with no gradual hierarchy, and cognitive disputation belongs to REBT/CBT; neither describes Wolpe's graded, relaxation-paired method. Mixes an exposure variant with a cognitive technique.","d":"Operant shaping and token reinforcement are operant-conditioning procedures for building behaviour through consequences, not classical-conditioning-based anxiety reduction. Confuses operant reinforcement with counterconditioning."}},"m2_the05":{"concept":"A token economy is a behavioural system in which desired behaviours earn tokens (secondary/conditioned reinforcers) that can later be exchanged for backup reinforcers such as privileges or goods, applying B.F. Skinner's operant principles at ward scale. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, in their 1968 work on chronic psychiatric inpatients, pioneered the systematic token economy (option b).","traps":{"a":"Aversion therapy reduces behaviour by pairing it with an unpleasant stimulus and was not the Ayllon-Azrin contribution. Names a different behavioural intervention entirely.","c":"Time-out (removing access to reinforcement for a period) is an operant technique but is not what Ayllon and Azrin are credited with pioneering on inpatient wards. Picks a related operant procedure that is not the specific innovation asked about.","d":"Modelling, learning by observing another person, is Albert Bandura's social-learning contribution, not Ayllon and Azrin's. Attributes a social-learning method to behaviour-analytic researchers."}},"m2_the06":{"concept":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) was created by Marsha Linehan and set out in 1993 specifically for chronically suicidal clients who met criteria for borderline personality disorder. It synthesises change-oriented behavioural strategies with acceptance drawn from Zen mindfulness, delivered through individual therapy, a skills group, phone coaching and a therapist consultation team (option c).","traps":{"a":"Schizophrenia is primarily managed with antipsychotic medication plus interventions like CBTp, family therapy and social-skills training, not DBT. Confuses the target population; DBT was not built for psychosis.","b":"OCD is treated first-line with exposure and response prevention, a specific CBT protocol, not DBT. Names a disorder with its own established behavioural treatment.","d":"Anorexia nervosa has its own evidence-based approaches (family-based treatment, CBT-E); although DBT is sometimes adapted for eating disorders, it was not developed for anorexia. Confuses a later adaptation with the original primary target."}},"m2_the07":{"concept":"DBT skills training, designed by Marsha Linehan, is organized into four modules taught in the group setting: mindfulness (the core skill of nonjudgmental present-moment awareness), distress tolerance (surviving crises without making them worse), emotion regulation (understanding and changing emotional responses), and interpersonal effectiveness (asserting needs while keeping relationships and self-respect). Option b lists exactly these four.","traps":{"a":"Free association, dream work, interpretation and working through are classical psychoanalytic techniques (Sigmund Freud), not DBT skills. Substitutes a psychodynamic process for a skills curriculum.","c":"Object relations, transference, splitting and projection are psychodynamic concepts (splitting is central to Otto Kernberg's model of BPD); they describe how the mind works in that theory, not teachable DBT skills. Plays on both models addressing BPD.","d":"Schema analysis, mode work and limited reparenting are components of Jeffrey Young's schema therapy, a different integrative treatment. Confuses two modern therapies that both treat personality pathology."}},"m2_the08":{"concept":"Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR) is a trauma therapy in which the patient holds a distressing memory in mind while attending to bilateral stimulation (typically side-to-side eye movements), across a structured 8-phase protocol. It was developed by Francine Shapiro, who observed the effect in 1987 and formalised the method in 1989 (option a).","traps":{"b":"Edna Foa developed Prolonged Exposure for PTSD, a different trauma treatment based on imaginal and in vivo exposure, not eye movements. Confuses two leading PTSD therapists.","c":"Aaron Beck founded cognitive therapy and the cognitive model of depression; he did not create EMDR. Names a foundational figure from the wrong tradition.","d":"Marsha Linehan created DBT for borderline personality disorder, not EMDR for trauma. Swaps in another well-known therapy developer."}},"m2_the09":{"concept":"Prolonged Exposure (PE) is a first-line PTSD treatment that has the patient repeatedly confront trauma reminders through in vivo exposure (approaching safe but avoided situations) and imaginal exposure (revisiting and recounting the traumatic memory) to reduce avoidance and fear. It was developed principally by Edna Foa and colleagues (option a).","traps":{"b":"Patricia Resick developed Cognitive Processing Therapy (CPT), which emphasises restructuring trauma-related beliefs (stuck points) rather than sustained exposure; it is a distinct, also first-line PTSD treatment. Confuses the two main trauma-focused CBT protocols.","c":"Francine Shapiro developed EMDR, which uses bilateral stimulation, not the exposure-based PE protocol. Names a different trauma therapy and its author.","d":"Marsha Linehan developed DBT for BPD, not an exposure treatment for PTSD. Substitutes an unrelated therapy developer."}},"m2_the10":{"concept":"Albert Ellis's Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT) is built on the ABC model, which locates emotional distress not in events themselves but in the beliefs about them. A is the Activating event, B is the Belief (especially rigid, irrational beliefs), and C is the emotional and behavioural Consequence; therapy then adds D (Disputing the irrational belief) and E (an Effective new philosophy). Option c states this correctly.","traps":{"a":"Antecedent-Behaviour-Consequence is the operant/behaviour-analytic ABC used in functional behaviour assessment (rooted in B.F. Skinner), not Ellis's cognitive model. A classic trap because both are called 'ABC' but the B differs: Belief versus Behaviour.","b":"Attention, Behaviour, Cognition is not a recognised model at all; it just recombines familiar psychology words. A distractor built to look technical.","d":"Affect, Behaviour, Coping does not correspond to REBT; 'affect, behaviour, cognition' is sometimes used to describe components of attitudes, but that is a different framework and this option even substitutes 'coping'. Invents a plausible-sounding triad unrelated to Ellis."}},"m2_the11":{"concept":"Existential psychotherapy holds that psychological distress arises from confronting the unavoidable 'givens' of existence. Irvin Yalom, in his 1980 text <i>Existential Psychotherapy</i>, named four ultimate concerns that every person must face: <b>death</b> (our finitude), <b>freedom</b> (the terrifying responsibility that we author our own lives with no external ground), <b>isolation</b> (the unbridgeable gap between self and others), and <b>meaninglessness</b> (the absence of any pre-given meaning in a neutral universe). Option b lists exactly these four, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Past, present, future and eternity are ordinary temporal categories, not Yalom's concerns. This plays on a vague sense that 'existential' involves time, but Yalom's framework is about confrontations with existence, not tenses of the clock.","c":"Love, work and play echo Sigmund Freud's supposed markers of health ('to love and to work') plus a generic addition, not Yalom. Transcendence belongs more to Abraham Maslow's later hierarchy; none of these are the four ultimate concerns.","d":"Birth, life, death and rebirth is a cyclical or spiritual/religious motif, not existential-humanistic theory. It exploits the surface link between 'death' and existential thought while smuggling in reincarnation ideas Yalom never used."}},"m2_the12":{"concept":"In group psychotherapy, Irvin Yalom argued that change is driven not by one technique but by a set of interacting <i>therapeutic factors</i>, described in his <i>Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy</i>. His classic eleven include universality (realising one is not alone in one's struggles), instillation of hope, imparting information, altruism (gaining by helping others), the corrective recapitulation of the primary family group, development of socialising techniques, imitative behaviour, interpersonal learning, group cohesiveness, catharsis, and existential factors. Option b names several of these directly, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Free association and interpretation are core techniques of Sigmund Freud's individual psychoanalysis, not Yalom's group factors. The trap is pairing a real psychotherapy method with the wrong theorist and modality.","c":"Token reinforcement and shaping are operant behaviour-therapy procedures derived from B. F. Skinner, used in token economies, and have nothing to do with group process factors. It swaps a behavioural mechanism for an interpersonal one.","d":"Schema reconstruction and mode work are components of Jeffrey Young's schema therapy, an individual CBT-derived approach. This distractor borrows a legitimate modern term but from an unrelated framework."}},"m2_the13":{"concept":"Logotherapy is an existential approach whose name comes from the Greek <i>logos</i> (meaning), and it treats the 'will to meaning' (the drive to find purpose) as the primary human motivation. It was founded by <b>Viktor Frankl</b>, an Austrian psychiatrist and Holocaust survivor who described the approach in <i>Man's Search for Meaning</i> (1946); its techniques include paradoxical intention, dereflection, and Socratic dialogue toward discovering meaning. Option c names Frankl, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Irvin Yalom is a leading American existential therapist and author of <i>Existential Psychotherapy</i>, but he did not found logotherapy. The trap uses another existential figure to blur authorship within the same school.","b":"Rollo May was a central figure in American existential psychology (<i>Love and Will</i>, <i>The Meaning of Anxiety</i>), yet logotherapy specifically is Frankl's creation. Same-school proximity is the bait.","d":"Karen Horney was a neo-Freudian who theorised basic anxiety and the 'tyranny of the shoulds'; she has no connection to logotherapy at all. This is an out-of-tradition distractor."}},"m2_the14":{"concept":"Structural family therapy views the family as an organised system whose problems come from a faulty <i>structure</i>: the invisible rules governing who interacts with whom. Developed by <b>Salvador Minuchin</b>, it maps subsystems (spousal, parental, sibling) and the <b>boundaries</b> between them, which can be rigid (producing disengagement), clear (healthy), or diffuse (producing enmeshment), and it examines the family's hierarchy of authority. Interventions include joining, enactment, and boundary-making. Option b captures subsystems, boundaries and hierarchies, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Multigenerational transmission of anxiety is a hallmark of Murray Bowen's family systems theory, not Minuchin's. The trap swaps one famous family therapist's core idea for another's.","c":"Cognitive distortions are the target of Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy, an individual model of thinking errors, not a structural-family concept. It imports an individual CBT construct into a systems question.","d":"Paradoxical injunctions belong to strategic family therapy (Jay Haley, the Milan group), where the therapist may prescribe the symptom. This distractor names a rival family-therapy technique to exploit within-field confusion."}},"m2_the15":{"concept":"Bowen family systems therapy, developed by psychiatrist <b>Murray Bowen</b>, treats the family as an emotional unit and focuses on how much each member can maintain a clear sense of self while staying connected. Its signature ideas are <b>differentiation of self</b> (balancing individuality against togetherness, and separating thinking from emotional reactivity), <b>triangulation</b> (a tense two-person relationship pulling in a third to stabilise it), and <b>multigenerational transmission</b> (patterns passed down across generations), often mapped with a genogram. Option c lists exactly these, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Narrative externalisation ('the problem is the problem, the person is not the problem') is the technique of narrative therapy by Michael White and David Epston, not Bowen. It substitutes a postmodern approach for the systems approach.","b":"Strategic paradox and prescribing the symptom are strategic-therapy techniques associated with Jay Haley and Cloe Madanes. The trap offers a different family-therapy school as the answer.","d":"Solution-focused exception questions come from Steve de Shazer and Insoo Kim Berg's solution-focused brief therapy, which looks for times the problem was absent. This distractor names a legitimate but unrelated brief-therapy method."}},"m2_the16":{"concept":"Schema therapy is an integrative model that extends standard cognitive-behavioural therapy to treat chronic, characterological problems such as personality disorders, particularly borderline personality disorder. It was developed by <b>Jeffrey Young</b> in the 1990s and introduces early maladaptive schemas (deep, self-defeating life patterns formed in childhood), schema modes, and 'limited reparenting', blending cognitive, experiential, and psychodynamic elements onto a CBT foundation. Option b names Young, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Aaron Beck founded cognitive therapy, the parent framework that schema therapy grew out of, but he did not develop schema therapy itself. The trap exploits the lineage by offering the ancestor instead of the actual originator.","c":"Marsha Linehan created dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) for borderline personality disorder, a different third-wave treatment. Because both target BPD, the distractor banks on confusing the two therapies.","d":"Steven Hayes developed Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT), not schema therapy. It offers another third-wave founder to mislead."}},"m2_the17":{"concept":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) is a 'third-wave' behavioural therapy whose goal is <i>psychological flexibility</i>: contacting the present moment fully and, guided by one's values, changing or persisting in behaviour. It was developed by <b>Steven Hayes</b> together with Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson, is organised around the six processes of the hexaflex (acceptance, cognitive defusion, present-moment contact, self-as-context, values, committed action), and rests on Relational Frame Theory as its account of language and cognition. Option a names Hayes and colleagues, so it is correct.","traps":{"b":"Marsha Linehan developed dialectical behaviour therapy, another acceptance-based third-wave treatment, but not ACT. The trap trades on both being mindfulness-influenced behavioural therapies.","c":"Aaron Beck founded cognitive therapy, a 'second-wave' approach that challenges thought content rather than fostering acceptance of it. This distractor offers the earlier CBT tradition ACT reacted against.","d":"Albert Ellis created Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT), which disputes irrational beliefs; it predates and differs from ACT's acceptance stance. It names an even earlier cognitive pioneer."}},"m2_the18":{"concept":"The Premack principle, formulated by <b>David Premack</b> (1965), is a rule about reinforcement: a behaviour the individual performs frequently and willingly (a high-probability behaviour) can serve as a reinforcer for a behaviour they perform rarely (a low-probability behaviour). The everyday version is 'first eat your vegetables, then you may play'; access to the preferred activity strengthens the less-preferred one. It underpins token economies and behavioural contingency contracts. Option a states this exactly, so it is correct.","traps":{"b":"That punishment must follow behaviour immediately is a general principle of contiguity in operant conditioning, not the Premack principle. It is a true behavioural fact used as a decoy because both concern operant learning.","c":"The claim that continuous reinforcement is most resistant to extinction is both false and unrelated: intermittent (partial) schedules produce greater resistance to extinction, not continuous ones. This distractor pairs a schedule-of-reinforcement topic with an incorrect assertion.","d":"That two unconditioned stimuli cannot be paired is a garbled statement about classical conditioning terminology, not operant reinforcement. It exploits confusion between Ivan Pavlov's classical framework and Premack's operant one."}},"m2_the19":{"concept":"Albert Bandura's social learning theory (later social-cognitive theory) argued that people learn not only through direct reinforcement but by watching others. Its two hallmark constructs are <b>reciprocal determinism</b> (behaviour, personal/cognitive factors, and the environment continuously influence one another) and <b>observational or vicarious learning</b> (acquiring behaviour by modelling, as shown in the Bobo doll experiments where children imitated adult aggression). Later work centred on self-efficacy. Option b names both constructs, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Reciprocal inhibition is Joseph Wolpe's behaviour-therapy principle: an anxiety response cannot coexist with an incompatible relaxation response, the basis of systematic desensitisation. The trap relies on the shared word 'reciprocal' to mislead.","c":"Reciprocal altruism is a concept from evolutionary biology (Robert Trivers) about helping others who may return the favour, not a learning-theory construct. Again 'reciprocal' is the bait.","d":"Reciprocal interpretation is not a recognised psychological construct at all; it is a plausible-sounding invention exploiting the 'reciprocal' pattern of the other options."}},"m2_the20":{"concept":"Free association is the instruction that the patient say aloud whatever enters the mind, without selection, judgement, or censorship, however trivial or embarrassing. It is the central technique of <b>psychoanalysis</b>, made its 'fundamental rule' by <b>Sigmund Freud</b> after he abandoned hypnosis; the analyst listens for resistances, slips, and transference to reach unconscious material. Option b correctly attributes it to Freudian psychoanalysis.","traps":{"a":"Behaviour therapy uses techniques such as exposure, systematic desensitisation, and reinforcement, all rooted in learning theory, not free association. The trap swaps an insight-oriented method for an action-oriented school.","c":"Person-centred therapy (Carl Rogers) relies on empathy, unconditional positive regard, and genuineness, not on free association. It offers a humanistic method in place of the psychoanalytic one.","d":"Cognitive therapy (Aaron Beck) uses guided discovery, thought records, and Socratic questioning to examine beliefs, which is directed rather than free. This distractor names a structured approach opposite to unstructured free association."}},"m2_the21":{"concept":"A therapeutic community is an inpatient model in which the ward itself, its daily meetings, shared decisions, and peer confrontation, is used as the treatment, so patients learn through the social environment. <b>Maxwell Jones</b> pioneered this democratic model at Belmont Hospital (later the Henderson Hospital), applying it to personality disorder and addiction through social learning and communal responsibility. Option a correctly links Jones to the therapeutic community.","traps":{"b":"The token economy is an operant behaviour-therapy system where tokens reinforce target behaviours, associated with Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, not Maxwell Jones. It offers a different institutional intervention as a lookalike.","c":"Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour with an unpleasant stimulus to suppress it, a classical-conditioning technique unconnected to Jones. The trap names another treatment method to test recall of who did what.","d":"EMDR (Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing) is a trauma therapy developed by Francine Shapiro, wholly unrelated to therapeutic communities. This is an out-of-era, out-of-field distractor."}},"m2_the22":{"concept":"Cognitive therapy proposes that emotional disorders are maintained by distorted thinking, and it teaches patients to identify and test these thoughts. <b>Aaron Beck</b> developed it first for <b>depression</b>, publishing <i>Cognitive Therapy of Depression</i> (1979) after noticing that depressed patients showed automatic negative thoughts and the 'cognitive triad' (negative views of self, world, and future). Only later was it extended to anxiety, personality disorders, psychosis, and substance use. Option d, depression, is therefore correct.","traps":{"a":"Schizophrenia was a much later application of cognitive therapy (CBT for psychosis), not the disorder Beck originally targeted. The trap offers a real but subsequent use to catch those who forget the chronology.","b":"Substance use disorders were also a later extension of the model, not its origin. It banks on the fact that CBT is now used there too.","c":"OCD is treated with cognitive-behavioural methods (notably exposure and response prevention), but it was not the founding focus of Beck's cognitive therapy. This distractor exploits the general familiarity of CBT for anxiety-spectrum conditions."}},"m2_the23":{"concept":"Response prevention means blocking the compulsive ritual a patient normally performs to reduce anxiety after a triggering thought. It is one half of Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP), developed by Victor Meyer (1966) and refined by Edna Foa and Gail Steketee, the first-line psychological treatment for Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder. In OCD the compulsion (e.g. handwashing) is negatively reinforced because it briefly lowers anxiety; by exposing the patient to the obsessional trigger while preventing the ritual, anxiety habituates on its own and the feared catastrophe fails to occur, disconfirming the belief. So OCD is the disorder in which response prevention is the defining, central technique.","traps":{"b":"Specific phobia is treated by graded exposure or systematic desensitization, but there is no ritual to block, so response prevention is not the central element. Trap: exposure is used for both phobia and OCD, but the 'response prevention' component specifically targets compulsions, which phobias lack.","c":"Depression is treated with behavioural activation, cognitive restructuring, or medication, not by preventing a compulsive response. Trap: picks a common disorder with no conceptual link to the technique named.","d":"Insomnia is treated with stimulus control, sleep restriction, and CBT-I, not response prevention. Trap: 'stimulus control' sounds behavioural and may be confused with response prevention, but they are distinct techniques for different problems."}},"m2_the24":{"concept":"In B.F. Skinner's operant conditioning, consequences are classified on two axes: positive means a stimulus is added, negative means a stimulus is removed; reinforcement always increases the behaviour, punishment always decreases it. Negative reinforcement therefore means removing an aversive (unpleasant) stimulus contingent on a behaviour, which increases that behaviour, for example a car buzzer that stops when you buckle the seatbelt, so buckling is strengthened. Option (a) states exactly this: remove an aversive stimulus to increase the behaviour.","traps":{"b":"Adding a pleasant stimulus to increase behaviour is positive reinforcement (e.g. giving praise). Trap: it is reinforcement, but 'positive', not 'negative'; confuses the add/remove axis.","c":"Removing a pleasant stimulus to decrease behaviour is negative punishment (e.g. taking away a phone). Trap: it correctly involves removal, but it decreases behaviour, so it is punishment, not reinforcement.","d":"Adding an aversive stimulus to decrease behaviour is positive punishment (e.g. a spanking). Trap: this is the classic confusion, calling any aversive event 'negative reinforcement'; adding an aversive to reduce behaviour is punishment."}},"m2_the25":{"concept":"Motivational Interviewing (MI) is a collaborative, person-centred counselling style for strengthening a person's own motivation and commitment to change by exploring and resolving ambivalence. It was developed by William Miller and Stephen Rollnick, originally in the addictions field, and its spirit rests on partnership, acceptance, compassion, and evocation, drawing out the client's own 'change talk' rather than imposing arguments. Therefore option (d), Miller and Rollnick, is correct.","traps":{"a":"Albert Bandura developed social learning theory and the concept of self-efficacy, not MI. Trap: self-efficacy is relevant to motivation, so Bandura is a plausible-sounding distractor, but he did not create MI.","b":"James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente created the Transtheoretical Model (Stages of Change: precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action, maintenance), which is often paired with MI but is a separate model. Trap: MI and Stages of Change are frequently taught together, so their authors are swapped.","c":"G. Alan Marlatt developed Relapse Prevention and work on harm reduction in addictions. Trap: same addictions field as MI, making him a tempting but incorrect attribution."}},"m2_bst01":{"concept":"In null-hypothesis significance testing the null hypothesis (H0) states there is no effect or no difference. A Type I error occurs when you reject a true null, that is, you conclude there is an effect when in reality there is none, a false positive; its probability is the significance level alpha. A Type II error (beta) is the opposite, retaining a false null (a false negative), and power equals 1 minus beta. Option (b), rejecting H0 when it is actually true, is the definition of a Type I error.","traps":{"a":"Retaining H0 when it is false (a false negative) is a Type II error, not Type I. Trap: swaps the two error types, the single most common confusion in this topic.","c":"Using too small a sample reduces power and raises the Type II error rate; it is not itself a Type I error. Trap: names a real cause of error but mislabels which error.","d":"Using a one-tailed test inappropriately is a methodological mistake that can inflate Type I error risk, but it is not the definition of a Type I error. Trap: associates a related procedure with the term rather than defining it."}},"m2_bst02":{"concept":"Statistical power is the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis, that is detecting a real effect, and equals 1 minus beta. Power is a joint function of four things: the true effect size (bigger is easier to detect), the sample size n (larger gives more precision), the alpha level (a larger alpha makes rejection easier), and variability (less noise means more power). Option (d) is correct because increasing effect size, sample size, and alpha all raise power simultaneously.","traps":{"a":"Decreasing sample size reduces precision and therefore lowers power. Trap: states the opposite direction of the true relationship.","b":"Setting alpha more conservatively (from .05 to .01) makes rejection harder, cutting Type I error but also reducing power. Trap: a stricter test feels 'better', but it costs power.","c":"Increasing variability adds noise that masks the effect, decreasing power. Trap: confuses more spread with more information; it is the reverse."}},"m2_bst03":{"concept":"Cohen's d is a standardized effect size for the difference between two means, expressed in standard-deviation units (the mean difference divided by the pooled standard deviation). Jacob Cohen proposed conventional benchmarks: d ≈ 0.2 is small, 0.5 is medium, and 0.8 is large. A d of 0.5 therefore falls at the medium benchmark, so option (d) is correct.","traps":{"a":"Small corresponds to d ≈ 0.2, not 0.5. Trap: undershoots by one benchmark.","b":"Negligible would be a d well below 0.2, near zero. Trap: dismisses a moderate, meaningful effect.","c":"Large corresponds to d ≈ 0.8. Trap: overshoots the 0.5 value by one benchmark."}},"m2_bst04":{"concept":"Non-parametric tests do not assume the data are normally distributed and typically work on ranks, used when parametric assumptions fail or data are ordinal. Each parametric test has a rank-based counterpart: the Mann-Whitney U test compares two independent groups and is the non-parametric equivalent of the independent-samples t-test. Option (a) is correct.","traps":{"b":"The non-parametric equivalent of the paired t-test is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test, not Mann-Whitney U. Trap: both are 'Wilcoxon/Mann-Whitney' style rank tests, easily swapped; Mann-Whitney is for independent, Wilcoxon signed-rank for paired.","c":"The non-parametric equivalent of one-way ANOVA is the Kruskal-Wallis test. Trap: extends beyond two groups, which Mann-Whitney does not handle.","d":"Chi-square is already a non-parametric test for categorical association and is not what Mann-Whitney replaces. Trap: names another non-parametric test as if it were the parametric original."}},"m2_bst05":{"concept":"In diagnostic testing, sensitivity is the ability of a test to correctly identify those who truly have the disease, calculated as true positives divided by all who actually have the disease, that is TP/(TP+FN). A highly sensitive test misses few real cases, so a negative result helps rule the disease out (the SnNout rule). Option (d), TP/(TP+FN), is the correct formula.","traps":{"a":"TN/(TN+FN) is the negative predictive value (NPV), the chance a negative test truly means no disease. Trap: uses true negatives, which belong to specificity/NPV, not sensitivity.","b":"TN/(TN+FP) is specificity, the ability to correctly identify those without the disease. Trap: the exact mirror concept of sensitivity, the classic swap.","c":"TP/(TP+FP) is the positive predictive value (PPV), the chance a positive test truly means disease. Trap: also uses true positives but divides by test-positives, not disease-positives."}},"m2_bst06":{"concept":"When two raters classify the same items into categories, some agreement will happen by chance alone; Cohen's kappa measures how much their agreement exceeds what chance would predict. It is therefore a chance-corrected index of inter-rater (inter-observer) agreement for categorical data, ranging from below 0 (worse than chance) to 1 (perfect). Option (a) is correct.","traps":{"b":"Internal consistency (how well items on a scale hang together) is measured by Cronbach's alpha, not kappa. Trap: both are reliability indices, but alpha is for item consistency, kappa is for rater agreement.","c":"Effect size for ANOVA is measured by eta-squared or omega-squared. Trap: unrelated statistic borrowed from a different family.","d":"Convergent validity (agreement between measures of the same construct) is assessed by correlations, not kappa. Trap: confuses agreement between raters with agreement between different instruments/constructs."}},"m2_bst07":{"concept":"A confidence interval is a frequentist tool, and 'confidence' is a property of the long-run procedure, not of any single interval. A 95% CI means that if you repeated the sampling and interval-construction process many times, about 95% of the resulting intervals would contain the true population mean. Once a specific interval is computed, the true mean is either in it or not; the 95% refers to the method's success rate. Option (b) captures this correctly.","traps":{"a":"Saying there is a 95% probability the population mean falls in this particular interval is the Bayesian credible-interval interpretation, not the frequentist CI. Trap: the single most common misreading; the fixed parameter has no probability under frequentism.","c":"95% of individual observations falling within the interval describes a reference range or tolerance interval, not a confidence interval for the mean. Trap: confuses spread of raw data with precision of the mean estimate.","d":"A CI is not a statement about the mean being 'significant'; significance concerns hypothesis tests. Trap: conflates estimation with testing."}},"m2_bst08":{"concept":"The p-value is a conditional probability calculated assuming the null hypothesis is true: it is the probability of obtaining the observed result, or one more extreme, given H0. A small p-value means the data would be surprising if there were truly no effect, which is evidence against H0, but it is not the probability that H0 is true. Option (b) states this correctly.","traps":{"a":"The probability that H0 is true would be P(H0 | data), which the p-value does not give; that requires Bayesian reasoning. Trap: the classic inversion of the conditional, treating p as the probability the null is correct.","c":"The size of the effect is measured by effect-size statistics (Cohen's d, r), not by the p-value, which is heavily influenced by sample size. Trap: assumes a smaller p means a bigger effect.","d":"The probability that H1 is true is likewise not what a p-value reports. Trap: mirror of option (a), inverting the logic toward the alternative hypothesis."}},"m2_bst09":{"concept":"One-way ANOVA (analysis of variance) tests whether the means of two or more groups differ, where the groups are defined by the levels of a single categorical independent variable. It works by comparing variability between groups to variability within groups via the F ratio, F = MS_between / MS_within; a large F suggests real mean differences. Option (a) describes exactly this.","traps":{"b":"Association between two categorical variables is tested by chi-square, not ANOVA. Trap: substitutes a different analysis for categorical-by-categorical data.","c":"Correlation between two continuous variables is tested by Pearson r. Trap: ANOVA does involve continuous outcomes, but it tests group mean differences, not a linear relationship between two continuous measures.","d":"ANOVA compares means (using variance as the tool); it does not test differences in variance alone, which would be Levene's or Bartlett's test. Trap: the name 'analysis of variance' misleads one into thinking it compares variances rather than means."}},"m2_bst10":{"concept":"When you run many statistical tests at once, each carries its own chance of a false positive (Type I error), so the probability that at least one test is wrongly significant across the whole family of tests balloons far above the nominal 5 percent. The <b>Bonferroni correction</b> guards against this by dividing the target alpha by the number of comparisons k, so a result is only called significant if its p-value is less than alpha divided by k. This keeps the family-wise error rate at or below 5 percent, which is exactly what option c describes.","traps":{"a":"Backwards: Bonferroni is conservative and actually REDUCES statistical power (raises the chance of Type II error), because it makes the significance threshold stricter. Power is increased by larger samples or bigger effects, not by a multiplicity correction.","b":"Confuses correction for multiple testing with weighting for unequal sample sizes. Sample-size imbalance is handled by the test's own formulas or weighting, not by Bonferroni.","d":"Describes data transformation (e.g. log or square-root transforms) used to normalise skewed distributions. That is a preprocessing step, unrelated to controlling error across multiple comparisons."}},"m2_bst11":{"concept":"The <b>Number Needed to Treat (NNT)</b> tells you how many patients must receive a treatment for one additional patient to benefit (one bad outcome prevented) compared with the control. It is the reciprocal of the <b>absolute risk reduction (ARR)</b>, where ARR equals the control event rate minus the treatment event rate expressed as proportions; so NNT equals 1 divided by ARR, which is option a. A smaller NNT means a more effective treatment.","traps":{"b":"Uses relative risk reduction instead of absolute. 1 divided by RRR is not NNT; RRR ignores baseline risk and can look impressive even when the absolute benefit (and hence real NNT) is tiny.","c":"This is a rearrangement toward computing an event rate, not NNT. Relative risk times control event rate gives the treatment event rate, not the number needed to treat.","d":"Odds ratio minus 1 is a meaningless quantity for NNT; NNT is built from absolute risk differences, not from odds ratios."}},"m2_bst12":{"concept":"<b>Allocation concealment</b> protects the randomisation process itself: it ensures that the person enrolling a participant cannot know or predict which arm the next participant will be assigned to (achieved by central randomisation, sequentially numbered opaque sealed envelopes, etc.). If enrollers could foresee the next allocation, they might consciously or unconsciously steer sicker or healthier patients into a preferred arm, creating selection bias. Option d captures this exactly: preventing enrollers from knowing the upcoming allocation.","traps":{"a":"Describes blinding (masking), not allocation concealment. Blinding hides the assigned arm AFTER enrolment to prevent performance and ascertainment bias; concealment operates BEFORE assignment to protect the randomisation sequence.","b":"Sealing or locking the analysis dataset relates to data management and analysis integrity, not to how participants are assigned to arms.","c":"Hiding the identity of outcome assessors is a facet of blinded outcome assessment, again a post-randomisation measure, not concealment of the allocation sequence."}},"m2_bst13":{"concept":"<b>Intention-to-treat (ITT)</b> analysis keeps every randomised participant in the group they were originally assigned to, no matter what they actually did afterwards (dropped out, switched, or stopped taking the drug). This preserves the balance created by randomisation and gives a pragmatic, real-world estimate of a treatment policy's effect, which is why option a is correct. Excluding non-adherers would reintroduce the very selection bias randomisation was designed to remove.","traps":{"b":"Describes a per-protocol style exclusion of protocol deviators. Dropping deviators breaks randomisation balance and tends to overestimate efficacy.","c":"Analysing only trial completers is a completers-only (per-protocol) approach; it discards dropouts non-randomly and biases the result, the opposite of ITT.","d":"Analysing by treatment actually received is the as-treated analysis, which reclassifies participants by their real exposure and reintroduces confounding, defeating the purpose of randomisation."}},"m2_bst14":{"concept":"<b>Spearman's rho</b> is a rank-based correlation: it converts each variable to ranks and computes an ordinary Pearson correlation on those ranks, so it measures the strength of a monotonic (consistently increasing or decreasing) relationship rather than a strictly linear one. Because it uses ranks, it works for ordinal data and is robust to outliers and non-linear-but-monotonic patterns, exactly the conditions in option d.","traps":{"a":"Describes the ideal conditions for Pearson's r (two continuous variables, linear relationship, roughly bivariate normal), not Spearman's rho.","b":"Two dichotomous variables call for the phi coefficient (or a chi-square based measure), not Spearman.","c":"Purely nominal (unordered category) variables have no ranks to correlate; association there is measured with chi-square or Cramer's V, not Spearman."}},"m2_bst15":{"concept":"Both are probability sampling methods that first divide the population into groups, but they use the groups oppositely. In <b>stratified random sampling</b> you split the population into strata (e.g. age bands) and draw a sample from EVERY stratum, which increases precision by guaranteeing representation. In <b>cluster sampling</b> you split the population into many clusters (often geographic), randomly select only SOME clusters, and then survey all or many members within those chosen clusters to save cost. Option a states this contrast correctly.","traps":{"b":"Both stratified and cluster sampling are probability (random) methods; neither is inherently non-probability. This reverses their nature.","c":"There is a real and important difference: stratified samples across all groups for precision, cluster samples whole groups for economy, and their precision relative to simple random sampling runs in opposite directions.","d":"Cluster sampling does not guarantee a larger sample; sample size is chosen by the researcher. Cluster designs typically have LOWER precision (higher design effect) than simple random sampling for a given n."}},"m2_bst16":{"concept":"<b>Cronbach's alpha</b> estimates internal-consistency reliability, i.e. how well the items on a scale hang together as measures of one underlying construct; it ranges from 0 to 1 and higher is better. Conventional benchmarks place below 0.5 as unacceptable/poor, 0.7 to 0.8 as acceptable, and 0.9 or above as excellent. An alpha of 0.40 therefore signals poor internal consistency (option a), well short of any clinical standard.","traps":{"b":"High-stakes individual clinical decisions demand very high reliability, typically alpha of 0.9 or above; 0.40 is nowhere near acceptable for such use.","c":"Excellent internal consistency corresponds to roughly 0.9 or higher, not 0.40. This inverts the benchmark scale.","d":"Alpha measures reliability (consistency), not validity. A scale can be internally consistent yet measure the wrong construct, so a low alpha certainly does not indicate high construct validity."}},"m2_bst17":{"concept":"The correlation coefficient <b>r</b> ranges from minus 1 to plus 1; the sign shows direction and the magnitude shows strength. Using <b>Jacob Cohen's</b> conventional benchmarks, an absolute r near 0.10 is small, near 0.30 is medium, and near 0.50 is large. So r equal to minus 0.30 indicates a medium-strength negative (inverse) linear relationship, which is option b.","traps":{"a":"No relationship corresponds to r near 0. A value of minus 0.30 is a real, medium-sized association, not the absence of one.","c":"A perfect inverse relationship is r equal to minus 1, where every point lies exactly on a downward line. Minus 0.30 is far from perfect.","d":"Correlation of any size never establishes causation; a negative r shows only that the two variables tend to move oppositely, not that one causes the other."}},"m2_bst18":{"concept":"Reporting guidelines are checklists that tell authors what must be reported for a given study type so readers can judge quality. <b>CONSORT</b> (Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials) is the guideline specifically for randomised controlled trials, making option d correct. Each design has its own matching checklist, which is why the other options belong to different guidelines.","traps":{"a":"Systematic reviews and meta-analyses are covered by PRISMA, not CONSORT.","b":"Observational studies (cohort, case-control, cross-sectional) are covered by STROBE.","c":"Diagnostic accuracy studies are covered by STARD, not CONSORT."}},"m2_bst19":{"concept":"<b>Random assignment</b> allocates participants to groups by chance, so in expectation the groups end up balanced on every characteristic, including confounders the researcher has not measured or does not even know about. This is its unique strength: it protects against selection bias and confounding on both known and unknown variables, exactly option c. No other design feature can balance unmeasured confounders this way.","traps":{"a":"Measurement error is reduced by better instruments and reliable, blinded assessment, not by how participants are assigned to groups.","b":"Recall bias (differential accuracy of remembered exposures) is a problem of retrospective data collection; random assignment does not address how accurately participants remember things.","d":"Publication bias arises at the level of which studies get published, not within a single trial; it is countered by trial registries and reporting of null results, not by randomisation."}},"m2_bst20":{"concept":"A <b>p-value</b> is a continuous measure of how compatible the data are with the null hypothesis; it does not jump in meaning at any particular number. Values of 0.049 and 0.051 sit essentially on top of each other and represent virtually identical evidence against the null. The apparent gulf between them comes only from the arbitrary 0.05 cut-off (<b>Ronald Fisher's</b> convention, later hardened into an accept/reject rule by <b>Jerzy Neyman</b> and <b>Egon Pearson</b>), so option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"A p-value speaks to statistical evidence against the null, not to the size or clinical importance of an effect; neither 0.049 nor 0.051 tells you a difference is clinically meaningful.","b":"Treating them as two very different conclusions is precisely the dichotomisation error the question exposes; the underlying evidence is nearly the same.","d":"Replication failure refers to a separate study not reproducing a result; two p-values from the same analysis straddling 0.05 have nothing to do with replication."}},"m2_lang01":{"concept":"A synonym is a word with the same or nearly the same meaning. <b>Benevolent</b> comes from Latin roots meaning well-wishing, and it describes someone kind, generous, and caring toward others. <b>Compassionate</b> (option a) shares this core sense of warm, well-meaning kindness, so it is the correct synonym.","traps":{"b":"Malicious means intending harm or spite, which is the direct antonym of benevolent, not a synonym. The trap tempts you to pick a strong emotional word of the opposite polarity.","c":"Indifferent means uncaring or neutral; a benevolent person actively wishes others well, so indifference is not a match.","d":"Severe means harsh or strict. It carries a negative, stern tone opposite to the kindly meaning of benevolent."}},"m2_lang02":{"concept":"An antonym is a word with the opposite meaning. <b>Eloquent</b> describes speech that is fluent, expressive and persuasive, delivered with skill and ease. Its true opposite is <b>inarticulate</b>, meaning unable to express oneself clearly or fluently. Only option (d) captures that reversal.","traps":{"a":"'Articulate' means able to speak clearly and effectively, which is a near-synonym of eloquent, not its opposite. This is the classic antonym-question trap of offering a synonym to a hurried reader.","b":"'Persuasive' is part of the very definition of eloquent (convincing speech), so it is a synonym, not an antonym.","c":"'Loquacious' means talkative or wordy, which describes quantity of speech, not its quality or skill. Being talkative is not the opposite of being eloquent; it exploits confusion between fluency of expression and mere volume of words."}},"m2_lang03":{"concept":"A <b>non-restrictive</b> relative clause adds extra, non-essential information about an already-identified noun and must be set off by a comma on BOTH sides, like a parenthesis. Here 'who was anxious' simply adds detail about the patient, so option (d) with paired commas is correct.","traps":{"a":"A semicolon must join two independent clauses; 'who was anxious' is a dependent clause, so the semicolon is grammatically wrong. It mixes comma and semicolon punctuation illegitimately.","b":"This opens the clause with a comma but never closes it, leaving an unbalanced non-restrictive clause. A parenthetical insertion needs a matching comma at both ends.","c":"With no commas at all this becomes a restrictive clause, which is grammatically valid but changes the meaning (it specifies WHICH patient, the anxious one, versus others). The question asks for correct punctuation of the added-detail reading, so this is the wrong choice here even though it is not ungrammatical."}},"m2_lang04":{"concept":"A <b>collocation</b> is a fixed, conventional pairing of a verb with a particular preposition. The verb 'insist', when demanding that something be done, standardly takes <b>on</b> (or 'insist upon'). So 'insisted on a follow-up' in option (c) is the idiomatic form.","traps":{"a":"'Insist for' is not standard English; 'for' collocates with verbs like 'ask for' or 'wish for', not 'insist'. It borrows a preposition from the wrong verb.","b":"'Insist with' is non-idiomatic; 'with' would suit 'agree with' or 'comply with', not 'insist'.","d":"'Insist to' is incorrect; 'to' fits 'object to' or 'agree to', but 'insist' demands 'on'. This trap tempts learners who default 'to' before any noun of intention."}},"m2_lang05":{"concept":"The <b>third conditional</b> expresses a counterfactual (contrary-to-fact) situation in the past, using the structure 'If + had + past participle, ... would have + past participle'. Since the main clause is 'I would have called him', the if-clause must be 'If I <b>had known</b>', making option (d) correct.","traps":{"a":"'Have known' is present perfect, which cannot form the past-counterfactual if-clause; the third conditional requires the past perfect 'had known'.","b":"'Knew' (simple past) belongs to the SECOND conditional, which describes an unreal PRESENT ('If I knew his number, I would call him'). It mismatches the past 'would have called' main clause.","c":"'Would have known' wrongly places 'would have' inside the if-clause; 'would' is reserved for the result clause, never the condition. This is the frequent 'double would' error."}},"m2_lang06":{"concept":"Parts of speech classify a word by its function in the sentence. An <b>adjective</b> describes or modifies a noun. Here 'inconclusive' follows the linking verb 'was' and describes the noun 'evidence', functioning as a predicate adjective, so option (b) is correct. The suffix '-ive' typically marks adjectives (decisive, effective).","traps":{"a":"A verb expresses an action or state; the actual verb in the sentence is 'was'. 'Inconclusive' names a quality, not an action.","c":"An adverb modifies a verb, adjective or another adverb and usually ends in '-ly'; 'inconclusive' describes the noun 'evidence', so it cannot be an adverb.","d":"A noun names a person, place or thing; the noun here is 'evidence'. The related noun form would be 'inconclusiveness', not 'inconclusive'."}},"m2_lang07":{"concept":"In 'neither ... nor' (and 'either ... or') constructions joining subjects of different number, the verb follows the <b>proximity rule</b>: it agrees with the nearer subject. The subject closest to the verb is the plural 'patients', so the plural past-tense 'were' in option (c) is correct.","traps":{"a":"'Was' is singular and agrees with the far noun 'therapist' rather than the nearer plural 'patients', violating the proximity rule.","b":"'Are' is present tense; while it agrees in number with 'patients', it wrongly shifts the sentence out of the past. The correct past-tense plural form is 'were'.","d":"'Has been' is singular present perfect, disagreeing with the nearer plural 'patients', which needs a plural verb."}},"m2_lang08":{"concept":"<b>Homophones</b> are words that sound alike but differ in meaning and spelling. The reliable rule: 'affect' is usually a verb (to influence) and 'effect' is usually a noun (the result). The sentence needs a noun after 'a strong', so <b>effect</b> in option (b) is correct.","traps":{"a":"'Affect' is normally the verb 'to influence'; a noun is required after the article 'a' and adjective 'strong', so the verb form does not fit.","c":"'Effects' is the correct word but plural, whereas 'a strong ___' demands a singular noun. It fails on number agreement with the article 'a'.","d":"'Affected' is a past-tense verb or participle, not the noun the slot needs; it cannot follow 'a strong'."}},"m2_lang09":{"concept":"<b>Its</b> is a possessive pronoun and, like 'his' and 'hers', takes NO apostrophe. The apostrophe form <b>it's</b> is strictly a contraction of 'it is' or 'it has'. Since the sentence means 'the coat belonging to it', the apostrophe-free 'Its' in option (d) is correct.","traps":{"a":"'It's' means 'it is', giving the nonsense 'It is coat is wet'. This is the single most common its/it's confusion.","b":"'Its's' is not a word in English; you cannot add an apostrophe-s to an already-possessive pronoun.","c":"'Its'' with a trailing apostrophe is also invalid; possessive pronouns never take an apostrophe in any position."}},"m2_lang10":{"concept":"A synonym question asks for the closest meaning. <b>Laconic</b> means using very few words, terse and concise. The word derives from Laconia (the region of Sparta), whose people were famed for brief, blunt speech. The closest match is <b>concise</b>, option (d).","traps":{"a":"'Verbose' means wordy and long-winded, the exact OPPOSITE of laconic. This is the antonym planted to catch readers who reverse the meaning.","b":"'Reluctant' means unwilling; it describes attitude, not brevity of speech, so it is unrelated to laconic.","c":"'Eloquent' stresses persuasive, skilful expression, not shortness. A laconic speaker uses few words; an eloquent one may use many, so it confuses brevity with rhetorical skill."}},"m2_lang11":{"concept":"The <b>comparative</b> form compares two things. For a short one-syllable adjective like 'tall', the comparative is made by adding the suffix '-er', giving 'taller'. The sentence compares her with one other person ('her sister'), so option (d) 'taller' is correct.","traps":{"a":"'More taller' is a double comparative; you use EITHER '-er' OR 'more', never both. Combining them is a redundancy error.","b":"'Tallest' is the superlative, used to compare three or more items or to mark the extreme, not a two-way comparison with 'than'.","c":"'More tall' misapplies the 'more + adjective' pattern, which is reserved for longer adjectives (more beautiful); a one-syllable word takes '-er'."}},"m2_lang12":{"concept":"An antonym is an opposite. <b>Benign</b> means gentle, harmless, or (in medicine) a non-cancerous, non-spreading tumour. Its opposite is <b>malignant</b>, meaning harmful or, medically, a cancerous tumour that invades and spreads. Option (d) is the correct antonym; the pair is standard oncology vocabulary.","traps":{"a":"'Harmless' is a synonym of benign, not its opposite. The antonym task punishes readers who grab the first similar-meaning word.","b":"'Tranquil' means calm and peaceful, overlapping with the 'gentle' sense of benign rather than opposing it.","c":"'Cordial' means warm and friendly, again close to benign in tone; none of these three provide the required opposite."}},"m2_lang13":{"concept":"An <b>analogy</b> asks you to find the same relationship between the second pair as holds in the first. The relation here is Profession : Characteristic Action: a psychologist provides counselling. Applying the same relation, a surgeon performs an <b>operation</b>, so option (a) preserves the profession-to-action link.","traps":{"b":"'Hospital' is the surgeon's workplace, a profession-to-place relation, which does not match the profession-to-action pattern of the first pair.","c":"'Scalpel' is the tool a surgeon uses, a profession-to-instrument relation, not the action the analogy requires.","d":"'Patient' is the recipient of the surgeon's work, a profession-to-client relation. It breaks the parallel, since 'counselling' is an action, not a recipient."}},"m2_lang14":{"concept":"A modifier is a word or phrase that describes another part of the sentence, and it must sit next to the thing it describes. An introductory participial phrase (one starting with an -ing word like <i>walking</i>) attaches to the subject of the main clause that follows it, so whatever noun comes right after the comma is understood to be doing that action. In option a the phrase <i>Walking down the corridor</i> is immediately followed by <i>I</i>, so it is correctly <i>I</i> who is walking, and the modifier is properly placed.","traps":{"b":"This is the textbook <i>dangling (misplaced) modifier</i>: the subject after the comma is <i>the painting</i>, so the sentence literally says the painting was walking down the corridor. Grammatically wrong because the participial phrase attaches to the wrong noun.","c":"This 'fixes' the dangling modifier by making the painting the literal walker, producing an absurd, factually impossible sentence. The trap is mistaking a grammatically parseable sentence for a correctly meaningful one.","d":"The modifying phrase <i>walking down the corridor</i> is jammed between the verb and its object, garbling the sentence so it reads as though the noticing (or the painting) is happening while walking, with confused word order. It is awkward and ambiguous, not the cleanly placed version."}},"m2_lang15":{"concept":"A synonym is a word with nearly the same meaning. <i>Ephemeral</i> comes from the Greek <i>ephemeros</i>, meaning 'lasting only a day', and describes something that lasts a very short time before fading, like a mayfly or a fad. <i>Transient</i> means passing quickly, temporary, so it is the closest match.","traps":{"a":"<i>Eternal</i> means lasting forever, which is the exact opposite of ephemeral. This is an antonym trap that catches anyone who half-remembers the word as being about time without recalling the direction.","b":"<i>Genuine</i> means real or authentic, a matter of truthfulness, not duration. It shares no meaning with ephemeral and is a distractor with no semantic overlap.","c":"<i>Tangible</i> means able to be touched or physically real. It concerns substance, not how long something lasts, so it does not answer a question about duration."}},"m2_lang16":{"concept":"Tone is the author's attitude toward the subject, inferred from word choice and what they choose to emphasise. Here the writer concedes one positive fact (a statistically significant reduction) but then piles on caveats: a small effect size, a very high 35% dropout rate, and inconsistent replication. Holding a finding at arm's length while still not rejecting it is exactly <i>cautiously sceptical</i> (accepting the result exists, but doubting how much it means).","traps":{"a":"<i>Wholly enthusiastic</i> would require unqualified praise, but the passage is dominated by limitations and doubts. The word 'although' at the start already signals the author is about to undercut the positive, so enthusiasm is ruled out.","b":"<i>Dismissive</i> means rejecting the finding outright as worthless. The author does not do this; they grant that the reduction was statistically significant and merely question its strength, so dismissive overstates the negativity.","d":"<i>Indifferent</i> means having no interest or opinion. The author is clearly engaged and evaluative, weighing evidence carefully, which is the opposite of not caring."}},"m2_lang17":{"concept":"This tests the homophone pair <i>compliment</i> versus <i>complement</i>, which sound alike but differ in meaning. A <i>compliment</i> (with an i) is an expression of praise or admiration. The therapist is praising the patient's improvement, so 'gave a compliment to' is correct.","traps":{"a":"<i>Complimentation</i> is not a standard English word; the noun form of compliment is simply 'compliment' or 'a compliment'. This is a made-up-word trap that looks plausible because of the real verb 'complimented'.","b":"<i>Complement</i> (with an e) means something that completes or goes well with another thing, as in 'the wine complements the meal'. It refers to completion, not praise, so it does not fit a sentence about admiring improvement.","c":"<i>Complement of</i> carries the same 'completing' meaning as complement, plus the phrasing 'gave a complement of to the patient's improvement' is ungrammatical. Wrong word and wrong construction."}},"m2_lang18":{"concept":"Articles (<i>a</i>, <i>an</i>, <i>the</i>) signal whether a noun is general or specific. A superlative adjective (best, tallest, most) singles out one unique thing above all others, and because that referent is unique and identifiable it requires the definite article <i>the</i>. So 'the best psychologist in the city' is correct.","traps":{"a":"This drops the required <i>the</i> before the superlative 'best'; 'He is best psychologist' is ungrammatical because a singular countable noun with a superlative needs the definite article.","b":"<i>A best</i> pairs the indefinite article with a superlative, which is contradictory: 'a' implies one of many unspecified, while 'best' implies the single unique top one. The two cannot combine.","d":"This also omits <i>the</i> before 'best', and 'of the city' does not repair the missing article. A superlative still needs 'the' regardless of whether it is followed by 'in' or 'of'."}},"m2_lang19":{"concept":"An idiom is a fixed expression whose meaning cannot be worked out from the literal words. <i>To throw in the towel</i> comes from boxing, where a fighter's corner literally tosses a towel into the ring to stop the fight and concede. It therefore means to surrender, give up, or admit defeat.","traps":{"a":"<i>To begin a new effort</i> is roughly the opposite of the idiom's meaning; throwing in the towel is about quitting, not starting. This trap exploits not knowing the boxing origin.","b":"<i>To make a joke</i> has no connection to the phrase and is a random distractor; the idiom carries no humorous sense.","c":"<i>To clean up after a session</i> is a literal-minded misreading triggered by the word 'towel'. The idiom is figurative and has nothing to do with actual cleaning."}},"m2_lang20":{"concept":"An antonym is a word of opposite meaning. <i>Transparent</i> describes a material that lets light pass through so you can see clearly through it, like glass. Its true opposite is <i>opaque</i>, meaning it blocks light completely so nothing can be seen through it.","traps":{"a":"<i>Clear</i> is a synonym of transparent, not an antonym. This trap catches readers who grab the first familiar word without checking the question asks for the opposite.","c":"<i>Translucent</i> means partly transmitting light (like frosted glass) so shapes are blurred but some light gets through. It is an intermediate state, not the full opposite; opaque is the true antonym.","d":"<i>Visible</i> means able to be seen, which overlaps in feel with transparent rather than opposing it, and it describes an object rather than a light-transmitting material. It is not the antonym."}},"m2_lang21":{"concept":"When a conjunctive adverb such as <i>however</i>, <i>therefore</i>, or <i>moreover</i> joins two independent clauses (each a complete sentence), the correct punctuation is a semicolon before it and a comma after it. Option c does exactly this: 'The session ended; however, the patient lingered.'","traps":{"a":"This has the semicolon before 'however' but omits the required comma after it. The comma is needed to set off the conjunctive adverb from the clause that follows.","b":"Using only a comma before 'however' to join two independent clauses creates a <i>comma splice</i>, a classic punctuation error. A comma is too weak to link two full sentences here.","d":"This runs the two independent clauses together with no punctuation before 'however' at all, producing a run-on (fused) sentence. It needs at least a semicolon before the conjunctive adverb."}},"m2_lang22":{"concept":"Concise writing conveys the same meaning with the fewest necessary words, cutting empty filler phrases. Padding like <i>due to the fact that</i>, <i>owing to the fact that</i>, and <i>on account of the fact that</i> can all be replaced by the single word <i>because</i>. Option c, 'Because she was anxious, the patient could not sleep,' says everything the original does in far fewer words.","traps":{"a":"'Owing to the fact that' is itself a wordy filler phrase, just as bloated as the original 'due to the fact that'. It swaps one long-winded opener for another instead of tightening the sentence.","b":"'On account of the fact that' is another padded phrase, and it adds the clunky passive 'sleep was not possible', making it even wordier. It is the opposite of concise.","d":"'Sleep was unable to be achieved by her' is awkward passive voice that inflates a simple idea and even misuses 'unable' (sleep cannot be 'unable'). It is longer and clumsier than the direct version."}},"m2_lang23":{"concept":"This is a vocabulary-antonym cluster about how much a person talks. <i>Taciturn</i> means habitually silent, saying little, and reserved by nature. The other three options all mean the exact opposite, so taciturn is the answer.","traps":{"a":"<i>Loquacious</i> means very talkative or chatty, the opposite of reserved. It is the antonym trap in a set designed to test whether you know the direction of each word.","b":"<i>Garrulous</i> means excessively talkative, especially about trivial things. Like loquacious it describes someone who talks a lot, not someone who speaks little.","d":"<i>Voluble</i> means speaking fluently and at length, a ready and abundant flow of words. It too means talkative, opposite to the reserved sense the question wants."}},"m2_lang24":{"concept":"In the <i>active voice</i> the grammatical subject performs the action of the verb (doer + verb + receiver). In the <i>passive voice</i> the subject receives the action and the doer appears in a 'by...' phrase or is dropped. Option a, 'The team discussed the case,' has the subject (the team) doing the discussing, so it is active.","traps":{"b":"'The case was discussed by the team' is passive: the subject 'the case' is acted upon, and the doer follows in a 'by the team' phrase. The tell-tale 'was + past participle + by' marks it as passive, not active.","c":"'The case has been discussed by the team' is passive (present-perfect passive: 'has been' + past participle). The case still receives the action, so it is not active voice regardless of tense.","d":"'The case is being discussed by the team' is passive (present-continuous passive: 'is being' + past participle). It is grammatically correct but the case is the receiver, so it is not active."}},"m2_lang25":{"concept":"Reading comprehension here turns on the word <i>however</i>, which signals the author is about to qualify the preceding claim. The author states a strong argument (early intervention prevents most chronic psychosis) but then notes the supporting studies used small samples and had no control groups, both serious methodological flaws. Pointing out these flaws means the cited studies offer only <i>weak methodological support</i> for the argument.","traps":{"a":"'Confirm decisively' is the opposite of what small samples and missing controls do; such flaws weaken confirmation, they do not strengthen it. This trap tempts readers who fixate on the initial strong claim and ignore the 'however'.","b":"'Irrelevant' is wrong because the studies are the very evidence cited for the argument; they bear directly on it. Their problem is being weak, not being off-topic.","c":"'Refute entirely' overstates the case. The author says the evidence is methodologically weak, not that it disproves the claim; weak support is not the same as refutation, and the author never rejects the argument outright."}}}});
